section
-- E1: module

-- E1: public import ConLeche.Kernel.PropRead
-- E1: public import ConLeche.Kernel.Basis

-- E1: @[expose] public section

/-!

The definitions of the core checker that mention no monad, no
`CoreFns` record and no fuel: the delta step and its readers
(`unfoldDefinition`, `unfoldableHead`, `headHint`, `sameConstHeads`),
the literal shapes and their guards (`natLitToConstructor`,
`natLitStored`, `strLitToConstructor`, `strLitStored`, `litShapeOk`,
`rawNatLit?`), the certified `Nat` operations' names, recurrences,
reducts and pins (`natOpNames`, `natDivModNames`, `natOpEquations`,
`natOpResult`, `natOpStoredOk`, …), the structure-η fabrications
(`etaProjs`, `etaFabArgsE`, `andRescueSlots`), the rule
data (`etaRescueTable?`, `recFireComparands`), the ι case split
(`iotaCtorNeverProp`, `iotaCase3`) and the tower entry's readers
(`projGuardRows`, `ProjEntry.typeAt`).

Split out of `ConLeche/Kernel/Core.lean` at task #305 (prep): a
relational description of the core's moves — a relation over `Expr`
and `Env` — must name these without importing the executable bodies,
and every one of them is exactly what such a relation needs.  The
bodies (`whnfCoreBody`, `whnfBody`, `inferBody`, `defeqBody`,
the knot and the fuel constants) stay in `Core.lean`,
which re-exports this module, so every importer of `Core` sees the
names unchanged.  Nothing here was rewritten: the definitions keep
their names, docstrings, attributes and relative order.
-/

namespace ConLeche

/-- **Is the spine's head neither a λ nor a `let`** (nor a loose bound
variable)?  The io grade's claim of such a term, where the term is not
the point, is a membership of its io-inferred type (task #342 L18,
`IORel`): a hereditary claim arises only at an abstraction.  A term in
weak-head normal form that is not a λ has such a head. -/
def Expr.ioNeutral (e : Expr) : Bool :=
  match e.getAppFn with
  | .lam .. | .letE .. | .bvar _ => false
  | _ => true


/-- Is the expression headed by a stored constructor? -/
def isCtorApp (env : Env) (e : Expr) : Bool :=
  match e.getAppFn with
  | .const c _ =>
    match env.find? c with
    | some (.ctorInfo _ _ _ _) => true
    | _ => false
  | _ => false

/-- **A stored inductive's result sort, read where it is needed**: the sort
its stored type ends in.  `none` where the type does not end in one.
Nothing about it is stored. -/
def formerResSort (ty : Expr) : Option Level :=
  match ty.piResult with
  | .sort s => some s
  | _ => none

/-- A stored former that does not show its telescope. -/
def formerDefHeaded (cv : ConstantVal) : Bool :=
  (formerResSort cv.type).isNone

/-- **The family's result-sort zero-ness datum**, read off its type; where the
sort is not read, `ifAllZero []`, "zero at every valuation", which no rescue
passes. -/
def formerZ (cvT : ConstantVal) : PropWhen :=
  match formerResSort cvT.type with
  | some s => Level.zeronessOf s
  | none => .ifAllZero []

/-- Is a stored inductive's result sort, at the given level
instantiation, provably nonzero (official `is_never_zero`)?  The
official kernel's structure rescue (`to_cnstr_when_structure`)
requires this of the major's type; `majorToCtor`'s structure-η
rescue mirrors it (`Sort u` at a concrete level such as `Unit`'s `1` passes, the
parameter `u` itself does not).  The instantiated zero-ness datum is
unsatisfiable exactly where the instantiated sort is never zero. -/
def formerNeverZero (cvT : ConstantVal) (us : List Level) : Bool :=
  (Level.substPW cvT.levelParams us (formerZ cvT)).isNever

/-- **A constructor's family**: the former and the capabilities stored at
the constructor's `induct` (the head of its type's result, `CtorWF`).
`none` where no inductive is stored there. -/
def ctorFamily (find? : Name → Option ConstantInfo) (induct : Name) :
    Option (ConstantVal × IndCaps) :=
  match find? induct with
  | some (.indInfo cvT caps) => some (cvT, caps)
  | _ => none

/-- Unfold the (application of a) definition at the head, one step.
`none` when the head is not an unfoldable constant.  **Theorems are
opaque to reduction**: a stored `thmInfo` never unfolds, so whether a
declaration type-checks never depends on a theorem's value — this
anticipates https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14896 (theorems
become opaque to the kernel: `is_delta` stops unfolding them).  The
one known input whose typing needs a theorem to unfold, the arena's
`subject-reduction-redex`, is rejected (an accept-subset of the
reference kernels until that PR lands). -/
def unfoldDefinition (env : Env) (e : Expr) : Option Expr :=
  match e.getAppFn with
  | .const n us =>
    match env.find? n with
    | some (.defnInfo cv value _) =>
      if us.length = cv.levelParams.length then
        some (Expr.mkAppN (value.instantiateLevelParams cv.levelParams us)
          e.getAppArgs)
      else none
    | _ => none
  | _ => none

/-- May the delta step unfold `e`'s head — is it a constant whose
stored declaration carries a value at a matching level-parameter count
(the official kernel's `is_delta`)?  This is the *decision* the lazy
delta step takes; the unfolding itself is materialized only inside the
branch that consumes it (`unfoldDefinition`), never in both slots of a
match scrutinee.  By construction
`unfoldableHead env e = (unfoldDefinition env e).isSome`. -/
def unfoldableHead (env : Env) (e : Expr) : Bool :=
  match e.getAppFn with
  | .const n us =>
    match env.find? n with
    | some (.defnInfo cv _ _) => us.length == cv.levelParams.length
    | _ => false
  | _ => false

/-- The reducibility hint of the constant at the head of `e` (`opaque`
when the head is not a stored definition — a theorem included, which
never unfolds). -/
def headHint (env : Env) (e : Expr) : ReducibilityHint :=
  match e.getAppFn with
  | .const n _ =>
    match env.find? n with
    | some (.defnInfo _ _ hint) => hint
    | _ => .opaque
  | _ => .opaque

/-- Are `a` and `b` applications of the *same* constant (the lazy delta
same-head short-circuit: try level and spine congruence before
unfolding both sides)?  Mirrors the official kernel's
`try_eq_const_app`: both sides must actually be applications. -/
def sameConstHeads : Expr → Expr → Bool
  | .app f₁ _, .app f₂ _ =>
    match f₁.getAppFn, f₂.getAppFn with
    | .const n₁ _, .const n₂ _ => n₁ == n₂
    | _, _ => false
  | _, _ => false

/-- The constructor form of a `Nat` literal, one layer:
`n + 1` becomes `Nat.succ (lit n)`, `0` becomes `Nat.zero` (the
official kernel's `natLitToConstructor`, used on recursor majors). -/
def natLitToConstructor (n : Nat) : Expr :=
  match n with
  | 0 => .const natZeroName []
  | k + 1 => .app (.const natSuccName []) (.lit (.natVal k))

/-- The stored `Nat` declaration has the expected shape. -/
def natIndOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some (.indInfo cv _) =>
    cv.levelParams.isEmpty && cv.type == .sort (.succ .zero)
  | _ => false

/-- The stored `Nat.zero` declaration has the expected shape. -/
def natZeroOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some (.ctorInfo cv _ _ _) =>
    cv.levelParams.isEmpty && cv.type == .const natName []
  | _ => false

/-- The stored `Nat.succ` declaration has the expected shape. -/
def natSuccOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some (.ctorInfo cv _ _ _) =>
    cv.levelParams.isEmpty &&
    (match cv.type with
     | .forallE (.const c1 []) (.const c2 []) =>
       c1 == natName && c2 == natName
     | _ => false)
  | _ => false

/-- `Nat`, `Nat.zero` and `Nat.succ` are stored with exactly the expected kinds, level parameters and
types.  The model interprets a literal by iterating the `Nat.succ` value on the `Nat.zero` value and
reads those shapes off this; a certified operation's check asks it of the environment it runs at. -/
def natLitSupported (env : Env) : Bool :=
  natIndOk (env.find? natName) && natZeroOk (env.find? natZeroName) &&
    natSuccOk (env.find? natSuccName)

/-- Whether a `Nat` literal's constants are stored.  What they are is the declarations' own check
(`litShapeOk`), so this reads no type: it says only that a literal may be read at all. -/
def natLitStored (env : Env) : Bool :=
  (env.find? natName).isSome && (env.find? natZeroName).isSome && (env.find? natSuccName).isSome

/-- Do all constants referenced in `e` (including inside `fvar` type
annotations) resolve in `env`?  A `Nat` literal implicitly references
the `Nat` basis constants (and a `String` literal additionally the
string-support constants).  Checked once per declaration; keeps the
environment well-formedness invariant syntactic. -/
def Expr.constsResolve (env : Env) : Expr → Bool
  | .bvar _ | .sort _ => true
  | .lit (.natVal _) =>
    (env.find? natName).isSome && (env.find? natZeroName).isSome &&
      (env.find? natSuccName).isSome
  | .lit (.strVal _) =>
    -- a string literal implicitly references the `Nat` trio (its
    -- character numerals) and the seven string-support constants
    (env.find? natName).isSome && (env.find? natZeroName).isSome &&
      (env.find? natSuccName).isSome && (env.find? stringName).isSome &&
      (env.find? stringOfListName).isSome && (env.find? listName).isSome &&
      (env.find? listNilName).isSome && (env.find? listConsName).isSome &&
      (env.find? charName).isSome && (env.find? charOfNatName).isSome
  | .const n _ => (env.find? n).isSome
  | .fvar _ ty => ty.constsResolve env
  | .app f a => f.constsResolve env && a.constsResolve env
  | .lam ty body | .forallE ty body =>
    ty.constsResolve env && body.constsResolve env
  | .letE ty val body =>
    ty.constsResolve env && val.constsResolve env && body.constsResolve env
  | .proj s _ e => (env.find? s).isSome && e.constsResolve env

/-- Convert a `Nat`-literal major premise to constructor form, one
layer; anything else passes through. -/
def litToCtorIfNat (env : Env) : Expr → Expr
  | .lit (.natVal n) =>
    if natLitStored env then natLitToConstructor n else .lit (.natVal n)
  | e => e

/-- A `Nat` literal reading of a whnf'd expression: literals and the
`Nat.zero` constant (the official kernel's `rawNatLitExt?`). -/
def rawNatLit? : Expr → Option Nat
  | .lit (.natVal n) => some n
  | .const c [] => if c = natZeroName then some 0 else none
  | _ => none

/-! ## String literals

A string literal unfolds on demand to `String.ofList [c₁, …, cₙ]` with
each character built by `Char.ofNat` from a `Nat` literal — the
reference kernels' `strLitToConstructor` (lean4lean `Expr.lean`, nanoda
`expr.rs`), mirrored exactly.  The names below are *pinned* like the
`Nat` literal names: a declaration at one of them has exactly the expected
type (`litShapeOk`, checked at the declaration), which is what the model's
interpretation of a string literal reads its meaning off.  A string
literal in the input without its constants is a positively detected
unsupported feature: inference *declines* (exit 2), and so does the
constant-resolution guard's error path (`unresolvedConstsErrorL`). -/

/-- The constructor form of a `String` literal:
`String.ofList (List.cons.{0} Char (Char.ofNat (lit c₁)) (… (List.nil.{0}
Char)))` — the official kernel's `strLitToConstructor`, spelling as in
lean4lean (`Expr.strLitToConstructor`) and nanoda
(`str_lit_to_constructor`). -/
def strLitToConstructor (s : String) : Expr :=
  .app (.const stringOfListName []) <|
    s.toList.foldr
      (init := .app (.const listNilName [.zero]) (.const charName []))
      fun c e =>
        .app (.app (.app (.const listConsName [.zero]) (.const charName []))
          (.app (.const charOfNatName []) (.lit (.natVal c.toNat)))) e

/-- The stored `String` declaration has the expected shape
(`String : Type`, no level parameters; any constant kind). -/
def stringTyOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some ci =>
    ci.toConstantVal.levelParams.isEmpty &&
      ci.toConstantVal.type == .sort (.succ .zero)
  | none => false

/-- The stored `Char` declaration has the expected shape (`Char : Type`,
no level parameters). -/
def charTyOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some ci =>
    ci.toConstantVal.levelParams.isEmpty &&
      ci.toConstantVal.type == .sort (.succ .zero)
  | none => false

/-- The stored `List` declaration has the expected shape
`List.{p} : Type p → Type p`. -/
def listTyOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some ci =>
    match ci.toConstantVal.levelParams with
    | [p] =>
      (match ci.toConstantVal.type with
       | .forallE (.sort u1) (.sort u2) =>
         u1 == .succ (.param p) && u2 == .succ (.param p)
       | _ => false)
    | _ => false
  | none => false

/-- The stored `List.nil` declaration has the expected shape
`List.nil.{p} : ∀ (α : Type p), List.{p} α`. -/
def listNilTyOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some ci =>
    match ci.toConstantVal.levelParams with
    | [p] =>
      (match ci.toConstantVal.type with
       | .forallE (.sort u1) (.app (.const l1 us1) (.bvar 0)) =>
         u1 == .succ (.param p) && l1 == listName && us1 == [.param p]
       | _ => false)
    | _ => false
  | none => false

/-- The stored `List.cons` declaration has the expected shape
`List.cons.{p} : ∀ (α : Type p) (head : α) (tail : List.{p} α),
List.{p} α`. -/
def listConsTyOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some ci =>
    match ci.toConstantVal.levelParams with
    | [p] =>
      (match ci.toConstantVal.type with
       | .forallE (.sort u1)
           (.forallE (.bvar 0)
             (.forallE (.app (.const l1 us1) (.bvar 1))
               (.app (.const l2 us2) (.bvar 2)))) =>
         u1 == .succ (.param p) && l1 == listName && l2 == listName &&
           us1 == [.param p] && us2 == [.param p]
       | _ => false)
    | _ => false
  | none => false

/-- The stored `Char.ofNat` declaration has the expected
shape `Char.ofNat : Nat → Char`. -/
def charOfNatTyOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some ci =>
    ci.toConstantVal.levelParams.isEmpty &&
      (match ci.toConstantVal.type with
       | .forallE (.const c1 []) (.const c2 []) =>
         c1 == natName && c2 == charName
       | _ => false)
  | none => false

/-- The stored `String.ofList` declaration has the expected
shape `String.ofList : List.{0} Char → String`. -/
def stringOfListTyOk : Option ConstantInfo → Bool
  | some ci =>
    ci.toConstantVal.levelParams.isEmpty &&
      (match ci.toConstantVal.type with
       | .forallE (.app (.const l1 us1) (.const c1 [])) (.const c2 []) =>
         l1 == listName && us1 == [.zero] && c1 == charName &&
           c2 == stringName
       | _ => false)
  | none => false

/-- `natLitSupported` plus `String`, `String.ofList`, `List`, `List.nil`, `List.cons`, `Char` and
`Char.ofNat` stored with exactly the expected level parameters and types.  The model interprets a
string literal through the values of these constants and reads those shapes off this.  (The reference kernels only check
*existence* of `Char.ofNat` and `String.ofList`; the type pins are what
makes the interpretation well-defined, in the spirit of the `Nat`
literal guard.) -/
def strLitSupported (env : Env) : Bool :=
  natLitSupported env &&
    stringTyOk (env.find? stringName) &&
    stringOfListTyOk (env.find? stringOfListName) &&
    listTyOk (env.find? listName) &&
    listNilTyOk (env.find? listNilName) &&
    listConsTyOk (env.find? listConsName) &&
    charTyOk (env.find? charName) &&
    charOfNatTyOk (env.find? charOfNatName)

/-- Whether a `String` literal's constants are stored (`natLitStored`). -/
def strLitStored (env : Env) : Bool :=
  natLitStored env && (env.find? stringName).isSome && (env.find? stringOfListName).isSome &&
    (env.find? listName).isSome && (env.find? listNilName).isSome &&
    (env.find? listConsName).isSome && (env.find? charName).isSome &&
    (env.find? charOfNatName).isSome

/-- **The shape a declaration at a literal's name must have**: what a literal's meaning reads off
it, its level parameters and type.  Checked at the declaration, once; nothing at a literal's use
compares a stored type.  A declaration check cannot show a kind, so the three `Nat` names are read
at the kinds their guards ask for; what they are beyond their types is the pinned `Nat` block's
(`basisNamesOk`). -/
def litShapeOk (c : ConstantInfo) : Bool :=
  if c.name = natName then natIndOk (some (.indInfo c.toConstantVal {}))
  else if c.name = natZeroName then natZeroOk (some (.ctorInfo c.toConstantVal 0 0 natName))
  else if c.name = natSuccName then natSuccOk (some (.ctorInfo c.toConstantVal 0 1 natName))
  else if c.name = stringName then stringTyOk (some c)
  else if c.name = stringOfListName then stringOfListTyOk (some c)
  else if c.name = listName then listTyOk (some c)
  else if c.name = listNilName then listNilTyOk (some c)
  else if c.name = listConsName then listConsTyOk (some c)
  else if c.name = charName then charTyOk (some c)
  else if c.name = charOfNatName then charOfNatTyOk (some c)
  else true

/-! ## Structural-Nat literal acceleration

The official kernel accelerates the structural `Nat` operations on
literals (GMP-backed there).  Here the fast path is *certified*: an
operation participates only when its defining recurrence equations
hold by definitional equality — checked once, at its check: `checkDecl`
positively rejects a nonstandard definition under one of these names,
so *presence in the store is the certificate* (no runtime flag, no
re-checking; a reduction-time re-check would in fact livelock — the
certification's own `pred zero` equation re-enters the fast path).

The certification runs in the environment *before* the operation is
stored, on the equations with the operation's self-references replaced
by its stored definition value (`Expr.substConst0`): running it
after insertion would let the operation's own just-enabled fast path
discharge its all-literal-argument equations (`pred zero ≡ zero`,
`beq zero zero ≡ true`) vacuously — accepting definitions that
disagree with the fast path on those points, which is unsound.  The
install also pins the operation's and its dependencies' types to the
expected `Nat → … → Nat`/`Bool` shapes (`natOpStoredOk`): the model
reads the operations' function-space memberships off these shapes.

The environment model carries the matching semantic clause: a stored
definition under one of these names satisfies its recurrences, from
which meta-level induction over the literal yields the computed
value.  WF-recursive operations (`div`, `mod`, `gcd`) and string
literals are deferred. -/

def natPredName : Name := natName.str "pred"
def natAddName : Name := natName.str "add"
def natSubName : Name := natName.str "sub"
def natMulName : Name := natName.str "mul"
def natPowName : Name := natName.str "pow"
def natBeqName : Name := natName.str "beq"
def natBleName : Name := natName.str "ble"
def natDivName : Name := natName.str "div"
def natModName : Name := natName.str "mod"
def natGcdName : Name := natName.str "gcd"
def natLandName : Name := natName.str "land"
def natLorName : Name := natName.str "lor"
def natXorName : Name := natName.str "xor"
def natShiftLeftName : Name := natName.str "shiftLeft"
def natShiftRightName : Name := natName.str "shiftRight"
def boolName : Name := .str .anonymous "Bool"

/-- `eagerReduce` (`Init/Core.lean`: `def eagerReduce (a : α) : α := a`),
the gadget that sets the official kernel's eager flag (task #353). -/
def eagerReduceName : Name := .str .anonymous "eagerReduce"

/-- Is `e` of the form `eagerReduce _ _` — official `is_eager_reduce`
(`type_checker.cpp`): the head constant `eagerReduce`, exactly two
arguments, any universe levels. -/
@[inline] def Expr.isEagerReduce : Expr → Bool
  | .app (.app (.const c _) _) _ => c == eagerReduceName
  | _ => false
def boolTrueName : Name := boolName.str "true"
def boolFalseName : Name := boolName.str "false"

/-- Is `e` the constant `Bool.true` — the official kernel's
`is_constant(e, Bool.true)` (`type_checker.cpp:1097`): the name, no
universe levels. -/
def Expr.isBoolTrue : Expr → Bool
  | .const c [] => c == boolTrueName
  | _ => false

/-- The certified structural-`Nat` operations.  Six of them
(`add sub mul pow beq ble`) carry a literal fast path; `Nat.pred` is
here without one — it has no fast path (official's `reduce_nat` folds
nothing unary but `Nat.succ`), but `Nat.sub`'s recurrence
`sub x (succ y) = pred (sub x y)` names it, so its own recurrences
must be certified for `sub`'s literal fold to be sound. -/
def natOpNames : List Name :=
  [natPredName, natAddName, natSubName, natMulName, natPowName,
   natBeqName, natBleName]

/-- The WF-recursive operations with a *pinned-declaration* certified
fast path: at its check, `checkDecl` compares the stream's definition
against a vendored pin of the toolchain's own (helper-unfolded)
definition by definitional equality, and then checks the pinned
`Nat.ble`-guarded characterization certificates
(`ConLeche/Kernel/NatOpPins.lean`) like theorem declarations — without
installing them.  Presence in the store is therefore again the
capability: a stored operation under one of these names has passed pin
and certificates, or the install declined.  (The family: every pin-certified WF-recursive kernel-accelerated `Nat`
operation, `Nat.div` and `Nat.mod` among them; official folds no unary
operation but `Nat.succ`.) -/
def natDivModNames : List Name :=
  [natDivName, natModName, natGcdName, natLandName, natLorName,
   natXorName, natShiftLeftName, natShiftRightName]

/-- **The certified operations' names**: the fast path fires on them by name, so only a
definition may take one, and its job holds it to the pin; any other declaration at such a
name is refused (`checkConstantVal`, `reservedRecName`). -/
def natOpName (n : Name) : Bool :=
  natOpNames.contains n || natDivModNames.contains n

/-! ## The names the environment's own guards look up

`natLitStored` and `strLitStored` decide whether a literal may be READ,
by looking ten fixed names up in the store; `litShapeOk` says what a
declaration at one of them is.  Three of them (`Nat`, `Nat.zero`,
`Nat.succ`) are reserved basis names, so only the pinned `Nat` block can
fill them (`basisNamesOk`); the other seven — `String`, `String.ofList`, `List`,
`List.nil`, `List.cons`, `Char`, `Char.ofNat` — are ORDINARY stream
declarations, and a stream is free to install them (it must: that is how
a stream earns string literals).

What must not happen is a *recursor* taking one of these names: it would
never pass the shape test as what it is.  The block's check refuses such
a name outright (`blockRecNamesUnreserved`,
`ConLeche/Kernel/Inductives/BlockShape.lean`). -/

/-- The ten names the two literal guards look up: the `Nat` guard's
three and the `String` guard's seven. -/
def litGuardNames : List Name :=
  [natName, natZeroName, natSuccName,
   stringName, stringOfListName, listName, listNilName, listConsName,
   charName, charOfNatName]

/-- **A name no block RECURSOR may take**: one a literal guard looks
up, one of the certified `Nat` operations (whose presence in the store IS their certificate, so a
recursor stored under such a name would claim a capability nothing
certified), or `Bool` and its two constructors, whose stored entries the
certified operations' guards read (`natOpTyPinned`, `natOpGuard`; task
must not move a guard).  Type formers and constructors are NOT held to this — a
stream must be able to declare `List`, `List.cons` and `Nat.add` —
which is why the block install spends this predicate on the recursors
alone.  The basis names are `basisNamesOk`'s: a pinned block's own
recursor carries one, and no other declaration may. -/
def reservedRecName (n : Name) : Bool :=
  litGuardNames.contains n ||
    natOpNames.contains n || natDivModNames.contains n ||
    n == boolName || n == boolTrueName || n == boolFalseName

/-- The operations (transitively) involved in `c`'s recurrences. -/
def natOpDeps (c : Name) : List Name :=
  if c = natPredName then [natPredName]
  else if c = natAddName then [natAddName]
  else if c = natSubName then [natPredName, natSubName]
  else if c = natMulName then [natAddName, natMulName]
  else if c = natPowName then [natAddName, natMulName, natPowName]
  else if c = natBeqName then [natBeqName]
  else if c = natBleName then [natBleName]
  else if c = natDivName then [natPredName, natSubName, natBleName, natDivName]
  else if c = natModName then [natPredName, natSubName, natBleName, natModName]
  else if c = natGcdName then [natBleName, natModName, natGcdName]
  else if c = natLandName then
    [natAddName, natMulName, natBleName, natDivName, natModName, natLandName]
  else if c = natLorName then
    [natAddName, natSubName, natMulName, natBleName, natDivName, natModName,
     natLorName]
  else if c = natXorName then
    [natAddName, natMulName, natBleName, natDivName, natModName, natXorName]
  else if c = natShiftLeftName then
    [natSubName, natMulName, natBleName, natShiftLeftName]
  else if c = natShiftRightName then
    [natSubName, natBleName, natDivName, natShiftRightName]
  else []

/-- The defining recurrence equations of a structural-Nat operation,
over constructor forms with free variables `d`, `d + 1` (the equation
sides are binder-free). -/
def natOpEquations (d : Nat) (c : Name) : List (Expr × Expr) :=
  let natTy : Expr := .const natName []
  let x : Expr := .fvar d natTy
  let y : Expr := .fvar (d + 1) natTy
  let z : Expr := .const natZeroName []
  let s : Expr → Expr := (.app (.const natSuccName []) ·)
  let ap1 : Name → Expr → Expr := fun n a => .app (.const n []) a
  let ap2 : Name → Expr → Expr → Expr := fun n a b =>
    .app (.app (.const n []) a) b
  let bT : Expr := .const boolTrueName []
  let bF : Expr := .const boolFalseName []
  if c = natPredName then
    [(ap1 c z, z), (ap1 c (s x), x)]
  else if c = natAddName then
    [(ap2 c x z, x), (ap2 c x (s y), s (ap2 c x y))]
  else if c = natSubName then
    [(ap2 c x z, x), (ap2 c x (s y), ap1 natPredName (ap2 c x y))]
  else if c = natMulName then
    [(ap2 c x z, z), (ap2 c x (s y), ap2 natAddName (ap2 c x y) x)]
  else if c = natPowName then
    [(ap2 c x z, s z), (ap2 c x (s y), ap2 natMulName (ap2 c x y) x)]
  else if c = natBeqName then
    [(ap2 c z z, bT), (ap2 c z (s y), bF), (ap2 c (s x) z, bF),
     (ap2 c (s x) (s y), ap2 c x y)]
  else if c = natBleName then
    [(ap2 c z y, bT), (ap2 c (s x) z, bF), (ap2 c (s x) (s y), ap2 c x y)]
  else []

/-- The reduct of op `c` on literal arguments (`pred` ignores the
second slot): the computation itself, at any size.  The official
kernel's maximum `Nat` size, which refuses a computation that would be too
large, is the cached core's (`natOpRefusal`,
`ConLeche/Cached/StateC.lean`). -/
def natOpResult (c : Name) (a b : Nat) : Option Expr :=
  if c = natPredName then some (.lit (.natVal (a - 1)))
  else if c = natAddName then some (.lit (.natVal (a + b)))
  else if c = natSubName then some (.lit (.natVal (a - b)))
  else if c = natMulName then some (.lit (.natVal (a * b)))
  else if c = natPowName then some (.lit (.natVal (a ^ b)))
  else if c = natDivName then some (.lit (.natVal (a / b)))
  else if c = natModName then some (.lit (.natVal (a % b)))
  else if c = natGcdName then some (.lit (.natVal (Nat.gcd a b)))
  else if c = natLandName then some (.lit (.natVal (Nat.land a b)))
  else if c = natLorName then some (.lit (.natVal (Nat.lor a b)))
  else if c = natXorName then some (.lit (.natVal (Nat.xor a b)))
  else if c = natShiftLeftName then
    some (.lit (.natVal (Nat.shiftLeft a b)))
  else if c = natShiftRightName then
    some (.lit (.natVal (Nat.shiftRight a b)))
  else if c = natBeqName then
    some (.const (if a = b then boolTrueName else boolFalseName) [])
  else if c = natBleName then
    some (.const (if a <= b then boolTrueName else boolFalseName) [])
  else none

/-- Stored-constant guards for op `c`: the `Nat` basis, every
dependency stored as a definition, and (for the `Bool`-valued ops and
the `ble`-guarded `div`/`mod`, whose semantic clauses mention the
`Bool` constructor values) the `Bool` constructors stored, level-free,
at the type `Bool` itself (their values must be members of `Bool`'s,
so that a recurrence `beq 0 0 = true` relates two members of one
type). -/
def natOpGuard (env : Env) (c : Name) : Bool :=
  natLitSupported env &&
  (natOpDeps c).all (fun n => match env.find? n with
    | some (.defnInfo cv _ _) => cv.levelParams.isEmpty
    | _ => false) &&
  (if c = natBeqName || c = natBleName || natDivModNames.contains c then
    (match env.find? boolTrueName with
      | some ci => ci.toConstantVal.levelParams.isEmpty &&
          ci.toConstantVal.type == .const boolName []
      | none => false) &&
    (match env.find? boolFalseName with
      | some ci => ci.toConstantVal.levelParams.isEmpty &&
          ci.toConstantVal.type == .const boolName []
      | none => false)
   else true)

/-- Substitute the level-monomorphic constant `n` by `r` through an
application spine (the certification equations' self-references; the
equation sides are binder-free, so only `app` recurses). -/
def Expr.substConst0 (n : Name) (r : Expr) : Expr → Expr
  | .const c us => if c = n ∧ us = [] then r else .const c us
  | .app f a => .app (Expr.substConst0 n r f) (Expr.substConst0 n r a)
  | e => e

/-- Substitute the level-monomorphic constant `n` by the *closed* term
`r` everywhere, including under binders (the div/mod certificate
statements' and proofs' references to the pinned operation; `r` being
closed, no lifting is needed).  `fvar` annotations are not entered:
the substitution runs on closed input terms only. -/
def Expr.substConstAll (n : Name) (r : Expr) : Expr → Expr
  | .const c us => if c = n ∧ us = [] then r else .const c us
  | .app f a => .app (Expr.substConstAll n r f) (Expr.substConstAll n r a)
  | .lam ty b =>
    .lam (Expr.substConstAll n r ty) (Expr.substConstAll n r b)
  | .forallE ty b =>
    .forallE (Expr.substConstAll n r ty) (Expr.substConstAll n r b)
  | .letE ty v b =>
    .letE (Expr.substConstAll n r ty) (Expr.substConstAll n r v)
      (Expr.substConstAll n r b)
  | .proj s i e => .proj s i (Expr.substConstAll n r e)
  | e => e

/-- The pinned codomain of a structural-Nat operation: `Bool` (itself
stored level-monomorphically at type `Sort 1`) for the comparisons,
`Nat` otherwise. -/
def natOpCod (env : Env) (c : Name) (e : Expr) : Bool :=
  if c = natBeqName || c = natBleName then
    e == .const boolName [] &&
    (match env.find? boolName with
     | some ci => ci.toConstantVal.levelParams.isEmpty &&
         ci.toConstantVal.type == .sort (.succ .zero)
     | none => false)
  else e == .const natName []

/-- The pinned type of a certified `Nat` operation:
`Nat → Nat` for the unary `pred`, `Nat → Nat → Nat` for the arithmetic
operations, `Nat → Nat → Bool` for the comparisons.  The model reads
the operations' function-space memberships off this shape. -/
def natOpTyPinned (env : Env) (c : Name) (ty : Expr) : Bool :=
  if c = natPredName then
    match ty with
    | .forallE dom body =>
      dom == .const natName [] && natOpCod env c body
    | _ => false
  else
    match ty with
    | .forallE dom (.forallE dom2 body) =>
      dom == .const natName [] && dom2 == .const natName [] &&
      natOpCod env c body
    | _ => false

/-- Op `n` is stored as a level-monomorphic definition with the pinned
type. -/
def natOpStoredOk (env : Env) (n : Name) : Bool :=
  match env.find? n with
  | some (.defnInfo cv _ _) =>
    cv.levelParams.isEmpty && natOpTyPinned env n cv.type
  | _ => false

/-- Peel a `∀`-telescope along an argument list (the residual type of
a fully applied telescope). -/
def piResidual : Expr → List Expr → Option Expr
  | e, [] => some e
  | .forallE _ b, a :: as => piResidual (b.instantiate1 a) as
  | _, _ :: _ => none

/-- The fabricated projections of a structure-eta spine: the
`.proj T j b` nodes, one per field of the family's projection table
(the node is what the table types and reduces). -/
def etaProjs (T : Name) (b : Expr) (nF : Nat) : List Expr :=
  (List.range nF).map fun j => Expr.proj T j b

/-- The constructor shape official's `try_eta_struct_core` tests before
inferring anything (`type_checker.cpp:824-829`): the candidate's head is
a stored constructor applied to exactly its parameters and fields.
(`structEtaCertWith` re-reads the same head; this is the gate that
keeps the inferences behind it.) -/
def etaCtorShape (env : Env) (a : Expr) : Bool :=
  match a.getAppFn with
  | .const c _ =>
    match env.find? c with
    | some (.ctorInfo _ cnP cnF _) => a.getAppArgs.length == cnP + cnF
    | _ => false
  | _ => false

/-- **A partially applied η constructor** (official's `try_eta_struct_core`
since lean4#15373): the candidate's head is a stored constructor applied
to fewer than its parameters and fields, and its family (the record's `induct`)
is a stored η family whose projection table's constructor it is.  `some k`, `k > 0`
the arguments it lacks.  Read off the stored records alone, before any
inference, as official's `is_non_rec_structure` gate is. -/
def etaCtorPartial (env : Env) (a : Expr) : Option Nat :=
  match a.getAppFn with
  | .const c _ =>
    match env.find? c with
    | some (.ctorInfo _ cnP cnF T) =>
      if a.getAppArgs.length < cnP + cnF then
        match env.find? T with
        | some (.indInfo _ caps) =>
          match env.findTable? T with
          | some tbl =>
            if caps.eta = true ∧ tbl.ctor = c then
              some (cnP + cnF - a.getAppArgs.length)
            else none
          | none => none
        | _ => none
      else none
    | _ => none
  | _ => none

/-- **The field telescope of an η family's instance** (the opening of
official's `is_unit_like` field loop): the η constructor's type at the
levels `us`, its `nP` parameter binders dropped and instantiated with the
type's arguments `targs` in one pass (official's `binding_body` ×`nP`,
then `instantiate_rev`). -/
def unitFieldTele (cty : Expr) (nP : Nat) (targs : List Expr) : Option Expr :=
  (Expr.peelPis nP cty).map fun body => body.instantiateList targs.reverse

/-- **The shape of an instance in official's `is_unit_like`**, read off
the stored records: `w`, a head normal form, is an instance of a stored
η family (one constructor, no index, not recursive) at its parameters,
whose constructor's field telescope at the instance's parameters is
`tele` with `n` fields to check (`some (tele, n)`), or not (`none`).
The constructor and the field count are the family's projection table's
(official takes the constructor from the family's constructor list): a
structure-like family has a table whatever its field count, and the
table's constructor is a stored constructor (`TableHead`). -/
def unitLikeShape (env : Env) (w : Expr) : Option (Expr × Nat) :=
  match w.getAppFn with
  | .const S us =>
    match env.find? S with
    | some (.indInfo cvS caps) =>
      if caps.eta = true ∧ us.length = cvS.levelParams.length then
        match env.findTable? S with
        | some tbl =>
          if w.getAppArgs.length = tbl.numParams then
            match env.find? tbl.ctor with
            | some (.ctorInfo cvC _ _ _) =>
              (unitFieldTele (cvC.type.instantiateLevelParams cvC.levelParams us)
                tbl.numParams w.getAppArgs).map fun tele => (tele, tbl.numFields)
            | _ => none
          else none
        | none => none
      else none
    | _ => none
  | _ => none

/-- The eta-rescue fabrication's argument spine: the major's type's
arguments, then the major's projections (`etaProjs`). -/
def etaFabArgsE (T : Name) (targs : List Expr) (major : Expr) (nF : Nat) :
    List Expr :=
  targs ++ etaProjs T major nF

/-- **The telescope check of a spine** (task #342 L18): the
telescope's syntactic arity, nothing else.  It is applied to spines of
the subject (the projection fire, `projCert`; the recursor's own
telescope in `iotaRec`): where the redex is not the point, its spine
fits its head's telescope by the application slots of its own
invariant, and a reduction claims nothing of a redex that is the
point. -/
def teleArityOk (ty : Expr) (args : List Expr) : Bool :=
  (Expr.peelPis args.length ty).isSome

/-- Open the first `n` `∀`-binders at fresh free variables `0..n-1`
(each fvar's type is the binder domain, instantiated with the earlier
fvars).  Returns the fvars and the opened body. -/
def openPisAtFvars : Nat → Expr → Nat → Option (List Expr × Expr)
  | 0, e, _ => some ([], e)
  | n + 1, .forallE dom body, i =>
    let fv : Expr := .fvar i dom
    match openPisAtFvars n (body.instantiate1 fv) (i + 1) with
    | some (fvs, e) => some (fv :: fvs, e)
    | none => none
  | _ + 1, _, _ => none

/-- Does `bvar i` occur loose in `e`?  (Not through fvar type
annotations — the generated telescopes are fvar-free.) -/
def Expr.hasLooseBVar : Nat → Expr → Bool
  | i, .bvar j => i == j
  | _, .fvar .. => false
  | _, .sort _ => false
  | _, .const .. => false
  | _, .lit _ => false
  | i, .app f a => hasLooseBVar i f || hasLooseBVar i a
  | i, .lam ty b => hasLooseBVar i ty || hasLooseBVar (i + 1) b
  | i, .forallE ty b => hasLooseBVar i ty || hasLooseBVar (i + 1) b
  | i, .letE t v b =>
    hasLooseBVar i t || hasLooseBVar i v || hasLooseBVar (i + 1) b
  | i, .proj _ _ e => hasLooseBVar i e

/-- `hasLooseBVar` with the packed bound's cutoff (task #214, P4): a
node whose loose-bvar bound is at or below `i` has no `bvar i`, so the
walk stops there without descending, so `structUsedLater` calls touch
only the spine of a large telescope, never its shared instance towers.  Read as `hasLooseBVar`
by `Expr.hasLooseBVarB_eq` (`ConLeche/Verify/LooseBVar.lean`). -/
def Expr.hasLooseBVarB (i : Nat) (e : Expr) : Bool :=
  if e.bvarB ≤ i then false else
  match e with
  | .bvar j => i == j
  | .fvar .. => false
  | .sort _ => false
  | .const .. => false
  | .lit _ => false
  | .app f a => hasLooseBVarB i f || hasLooseBVarB i a
  | .lam ty b => hasLooseBVarB i ty || hasLooseBVarB (i + 1) b
  | .forallE ty b => hasLooseBVarB i ty || hasLooseBVarB (i + 1) b
  | .letE t v b =>
    hasLooseBVarB i t || hasLooseBVarB i v || hasLooseBVarB (i + 1) b
  | .proj _ _ e => hasLooseBVarB i e

/-- **Binder `nP + j` is used by the rest of the telescope**: its
variable occurs in the remainder of `cty` after it (a later field's
domain or the result).  The occurrence predicate the positivity check
and the model state their facts in. -/
def structUsedLater (cty : Expr) (nP j : Nat) : Bool :=
  match cty.stripPis (nP + j + 1) with
  | some (_, rest) => rest.hasLooseBVarB 0
  | none => false

/-- **The rows of a projection's guard: the field loop of the official
`infer_proj`.**  `r` is the constructor telescope with its parameters and
every field before the current one opened, the current field's variable
at depth `d`; `k` fields remain before the projected one.  A field the
rest of the telescope uses is a row, its domain at depth `d`, and is
opened at its variable; one it does not use is stepped over, as official
does.  The rest's only loose variable is the current field's, so
`hasLooseBVarB 0` is answered by the packed bound at the binder.  The
projected field's domain is the last row.  `none` where the telescope
binds too few fields. -/
def projGuardRowsGo : Nat → Nat → Expr → Option (List (Nat × Expr))
  | 0, d, .forallE dom _ => some [(d, dom)]
  | k + 1, d, .forallE dom body =>
    if body.hasLooseBVarB 0 then
      match projGuardRowsGo k (d + 1) (body.instantiate1 (.fvar d dom)) with
      | some rows => some ((d, dom) :: rows)
      | none => none
    else projGuardRowsGo k (d + 1) body
  | _, _, _ => none

/-- **What a `.proj` use on a `Prop`-declared structure must show**
(official `infer_proj`, task #480): the domains, at the use's level
instantiation and at the constructor telescope's own variables, that
must be propositions — the projected field's, and that of every earlier
field a later field's type uses.  The parameters are opened, then the
fields stepped (`projGuardRowsGo`).  Each row is a depth and a term at
that depth.  `none` where the stored constructor does not bind what the
entry says (the use is a reject).  Computed where it is read; nothing
about it is stored. -/
def projGuardRows (find? : Name → Option ConstantInfo) (entry : ProjEntry)
    (us : List Level) : Option (List (Nat × Expr)) :=
  match find? entry.ctor with
  | some ci =>
    let cv := ci.toConstantVal
    match openPisAtFvars entry.numParams
        (cv.type.instantiateLevelParams cv.levelParams us) 0 with
    | some (_, r) => projGuardRowsGo entry.idx entry.numParams r
    | none => none
  | none => none

/-- **`And`'s projection slots, ready to fire**: the two
tower entries of `And` are stored and name the rule's constructor at the
major's parameter count.  The gate of `majorToCtor`'s `And` branch;
abstracted over the lookup so the indexed twin
(`FEnv.andRescueSlotsF`) shares the body. -/
def andRescueSlotsOf (findProj? : Name → Nat → Option ProjEntry)
    (ctor : Name) (nP : Nat) : Bool :=
  (List.range 2).all fun j =>
    match findProj? andName j with
    | some e => e.ctor == ctor && e.numParams == nP && e.numFields == 2
    | none => false

/-- `andRescueSlotsOf` at the plain environment. -/
def andRescueSlots (env : Env) (ctor : Name) (nP : Nat) : Bool :=
  andRescueSlotsOf env.findProj? ctor nP

/-- **The structure-η rescue's standing condition, at use**: the single
rule's constructor `ctor` (stored as `cvj`) is the constructor of its
family's projection table `tbl?` (the family `cvT` structure-like,
`IndCaps.eta`), and carries the family's level parameters; the table, for
the fabrication's counts.  The rescue asks this where it has just looked
all of them up; nothing about it is stored.

The level-parameter conjunct is what lets the rescue fabricate the
constructor application at the major type's levels without comparing
the two lists per call.  `And.intro` is left to `And`'s own rescue. -/
def etaRescueTable? (tbl? : Option ProjTable) (ctor : Name) (cvj cvT : ConstantVal)
    (caps : IndCaps) : Option ProjTable :=
  match tbl? with
  | some tbl =>
    if caps.eta && tbl.ctor == ctor && cvj.levelParams == cvT.levelParams &&
        ctor != andIntroName then some tbl
    else none
  | none => none

/-- What `etaRescueTable?` says. -/
theorem etaRescueTable?_inv {tbl? : Option ProjTable} {ctor : Name} {cvj cvT : ConstantVal}
    {caps : IndCaps} {tbl : ProjTable}
    (h : etaRescueTable? tbl? ctor cvj cvT caps = some tbl) :
    tbl? = some tbl ∧ caps.eta = true ∧ tbl.ctor = ctor ∧
      cvj.levelParams = cvT.levelParams := by
  unfold etaRescueTable? at h
  split at h
  · split at h
    · rename_i h'
      simp only [Bool.and_eq_true, beq_iff_eq] at h'
      cases h
      exact ⟨rfl, h'.1.1.1, h'.1.1.2, h'.1.2⟩
    · exact nomatch h
  · exact nomatch h

/-- The level and constructor-parameter comparands a firing rule's
checks compare the major's constructor levels and parameters against:
for a `.plain` rule the constructor's levels link to the recursor's by
name and its parameters are the recursor's leading arguments; for a
`.nested` rule both are the stored instantiations (the class the
auxiliary recursor's type was generated from, `auxRuleFire`), at the
recursor's level instantiation and (for the parameters) instantiated at
the recursor's leading-argument spine (the stored pins live in the
`rP`-binder prefix context; index arguments never occur in them); a
`.levels` rule's levels are stored and its parameters a `.plain` rule's. -/
def recFireComparands (rl : RecRule) (cnP : Nat) (lps : List Name)
    (us : List Level) (cvjLps : List Name) (args : List Expr)
    (rP : Nat) : List Level × List Expr :=
  match rl.fire with
  | .nested lvls pins =>
    (lvls.map (Level.subst lps us),
     pins.map fun p => Expr.instSpine (args.take rP) (rP - 1)
       (p.instantiateLevelParams lps us))
  | .plain =>
    (cvjLps.map fun p => Level.subst lps us (.param p),
     args.take cnP)
  | .levels lvls =>
    (lvls.map (Level.subst lps us), args.take cnP)

/-- **How many comparand levels a rule has** (`recFireComparands`' first
component's length, read without substituting). -/
def recFireLevelCount (rl : RecRule) (cvjLps : List Name) : Nat :=
  match rl.fire with
  | .nested lvls _ => lvls.length
  | .plain => cvjLps.length
  | .levels lvls => lvls.length

theorem recFireComparands_fst_length (rl : RecRule) (cnP : Nat) (lps : List Name)
    (us : List Level) (cvjLps : List Name) (args : List Expr) (rP : Nat) :
    (recFireComparands rl cnP lps us cvjLps args rP).1.length = recFireLevelCount rl cvjLps := by
  unfold recFireComparands recFireLevelCount
  cases rl.fire <;> simp

/-- **A rule fires with no certificate, by the stored facts** (the ι case
split's case 2 read off the records, task #573): at a K recursor, or where
the constructor's family records a sort that is never zero
(`IndCaps.neverZero`) and has `n` level parameters, the comparand levels'
count. -/
def iotaNoCert (find? : Name → Option ConstantInfo) (isK : Bool) (induct : Name) (n : Nat) :
    Bool :=
  isK ||
    match ctorFamily find? induct with
    | some (cvT, caps) => caps.neverZero && cvT.levelParams.length == n
    | none => false

/-- **The type of a `.proj` node at a tower-backed entry** (task #175
S1): the stored body `F_i[p⃗ ↦ bvars, f_j ↦ .proj T j (bvar 0)]`,
level-instantiated at the subject type's levels, with the subject
type's arguments and the subject substituted for its `numParams + 1`
loose variables in ONE traversal (`instantiateList`: `bvar 0` is the
subject, `bvar (numParams - k)` parameter `k`). -/
def ProjEntry.typeAt (entry : ProjEntry) (us : List Level) (targs : List Expr)
    (pe : Expr) : Expr :=
  (entry.body.instantiateLevelParams entry.levelParams us).instantiateList
    (pe :: targs.reverse)

end ConLeche

end
-- ===== ConLeche.Kernel.Core (1921 lines) =====
section
-- E1: module

-- E1: import ConLeche.Kernel.Level
-- E1: import ConLeche.Kernel.ExprOps
-- E1: public import ConLeche.Kernel.CoreDefs

-- E1: @[expose] public section

/-!

Every core function is written **once**, as a non-recursive *body*
parameterized over a record of the mutually recursive entry points
(`CoreFns`) and polymorphic in the monad.  All recursion is routed
through the record — bodies never call themselves, and the few helpers
that do recurse (`iotaCertsFull`, `defEqList`) do so
structurally on a list.  Fuel lives only in the *knots* that tie the
record: the pure knot (`ConLeche.Kernel.TypeChecker`) instantiates the
bodies at `CheckM` and is the verification's subject; the cached knot
(`ConLeche.Cached.CoreC`) instantiates them over the cached
representation with the memo caches and is what the checker executes.
A refinement bridge relates the two (see DESIGN.md).

The reduction loop follows the official kernel (`whnfCore` never
delta-unfolds; `whnf` iterates `whnfCore → reduceNat → unfold one
definition`), and definitional equality starts with the syntactic fast
path and tries proof irrelevance before structural congruence.
Inference comes at two grades, as in the official kernel: the full
grade (`inferBody`) checks application arguments, λ domains and `let`
values; the infer-only grade (`inferBodyIO`, the knot's `inferIO`
slot) re-derives a type without those checks and is what reduction and
definitional equality call on their own intermediate terms.

Verification: `ConLeche.Model.*` and `ConLeche.Verify.*` (claims),
`ConLeche.Verify.*` (inversions), both stated against the bodies with
hypotheses about the record and discharged by one induction at the
knot.

The core's **fuel-free helpers** — everything a body reads that
mentions no monad, no record and no fuel (`unfoldDefinition`, the
literal guards, the certified `Nat` operations' tables, the η
fabrications, the block's rule bits, …) — live
in `ConLeche/Kernel/CoreDefs.lean` and are
re-exported here, so that a relational description of the core's
moves can name them without importing the bodies.
-/

namespace ConLeche

/-- **The verdict at a constant the environment does not know.**

`sorryAx` is the one axiom the checker tolerates as a *declaration*
and installs nothing for (`sorryAxName`,
`ConLeche/Kernel/Basis/Names.lean`): an export declares it whenever
its module mentions `sorry`, so the record is skipped and the stream
goes on — but there is no set model for it, so a *use* is a
positively detected unsupported feature and the run DECLINES, at the
record that uses it.  Every other unresolved name is a malformed
stream: a REJECT, with the message unchanged.

This decides the question wherever inference meets the constant
first.  Its companion `unresolvedConstsError`
(`ConLeche/Kernel/CheckerBase.lean`) decides the same question at the
guard that keeps unresolved constants out of stored terms
(`Expr.constsResolve`). -/
def unknownConstError (n : Name) : CheckError :=
  if n = sorryAxName then .notImplemented "use of the sorryAx axiom"
  else .invalid s!"unknown constant {n}"

/-- **Could official's `infer_proj` type `.proj sn i _` at a subject
of type `T a⃗` where we store no projection table for `T`?**  Only at a
single-constructor inductive `T` named by the node, applied to MORE
arguments than its parameters (official's premise is `#ctors = 1` and
`#args = nparams + nindices`, so indices are allowed), at a field index
below the constructor's field count: an INDEXED structure-like type,
where official projects and we store no table (the installer stores
one at every index-free single-constructor member and only there,
`blockTables`).  Every other shape official rejects
(`invalid_proj_exception`): a head that is no inductive (an axiom, an
opaque, `Quot`), a constructor count other than one, a node naming
another type, a field index beyond the fields (`PUnit`, `Eq`: no
fields; an index-free one-constructor type always has a table). -/
def projIndexedStructLike (find? : Name → Option ConstantInfo) (T sn : Name)
    (i nArgs : Nat) : Bool :=
  T == sn &&
    match find? T with
    | some (.indInfo _ caps) =>
      match caps.ctors with
      | [c] =>
        caps.nparams < nArgs &&
          match find? c with
          | some (.ctorInfo _ _ nF _) => decide (i < nF)
          | _ => false
      | _ => false
    | _ => false

/-- **The verdict at a `.proj sn i _` whose subject type is headed by
the constant `T` (applied to `nArgs` arguments) and has no
projection-table entry at field `i`** (`hasTable`: whether `T` has a
table at all).  A table without field `i` is an out-of-range index; a
positively detected indexed structure-like type (`projIndexedStructLike`)
is the one shape official accepts and we do not support (DECLINE, on
purpose: https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/issues/14977 removes
projections from indexed inductives); everything else rejects. -/
def projMissError (find? : Name → Option ConstantInfo) (hasTable : Bool)
    (T sn : Name) (i nArgs : Nat) : CheckError :=
  if hasTable then .invalid "projection index out of range"
  else if projIndexedStructLike find? T sn i nArgs then
    .notImplemented "projection on an indexed structure-like type"
  else .invalid "invalid projection: not a structure-like type, or no such field"

/-- The record of mutually recursive core entry points.  `whnfCore`
computes a head normal form without delta — its first argument is the
official kernel's `cheap_proj` flag (`whnfCoreBody`); `whnf` is the full reduction
loop; `infer` is type inference;
`defeq` is definitional equality; `inferIO` is inference at the
infer-only grade. -/
structure CoreFns (m : Type → Type u) where
  whnfCore : Bool → Nat → Expr → m Expr
  whnf : Nat → Expr → m Expr
  infer : Nat → Expr → m Expr
  defeq : Nat → Expr → Expr → m Bool
  /-- Definitional equality in the **eager tower** (task #353): the
  official kernel's `is_def_eq` with `m_eager_reduce` set, which
  `infer_app` sets for the comparison of an `eagerReduce _ _`
  argument's type with its domain (`defeqArg`).  The knot ties it to
  the bodies at `eager := true` one level down, so the flag holds for
  the whole comparison and everything it calls, as official's
  `flet` scope does. -/
  defeqE : Nat → Expr → Expr → m Bool
  /-- Type inference at the **infer-only grade** (task #170): the
  official kernel's `infer_type_core(e, infer_only = true)`, the entry
  every *internal* inference call site uses: the io body
  (`inferBodyIO`), which checks no application argument and no λ
  domain (task #346: its claim is hereditary on the type's syntax). -/
  inferIO : Nat → Expr → m Expr

/-- The **io-grade view** of a core record: the record whose full-grade
`infer` slot is the io slot, so that a body written against `r.infer`
recurses at the io grade when handed `r.ioView`.  This is how the io
inference body propagates its own grade (official: `infer_type_core`
passes `infer_only` down) without a textual twin. -/
def CoreFns.ioView {m : Type → Type u} (r : CoreFns m) : CoreFns m :=
  { r with infer := r.inferIO }

section Bodies

variable {m : Type → Type} [Monad m] [MonadExceptOf CheckError m]

/- The mode setting (`CheckMode`, `ConLeche/Kernel/Env.lean`):
definitions below that mention `mode` take it as their first explicit
argument (after the monad instances). -/
variable (mode : CheckMode)

/-- Lift a fuel-style partial result.  Its only client is the level
comparison (`Level.leq`/`isEquiv`), whose `none` is its fuel running out
(`Level.defaultFuel`) — OUR resource limit, so a DECLINE (exit 2), never
a verdict (charter item 9).  Every check that REQUIRES a comparison between two
levels neither of which is `zero` goes through here, so an exhausted
comparison cannot read as "not equivalent".  (A comparison against
`zero` cannot misread: `simplify` sends every always-zero level to
`zero`, and `isEquiv` answers `true` on that fast path, so its `none`
only ever stands for a true "no".) -/
def liftFueled (what : String) : Option α → m α
  | some a => pure a
  | none => throw (.notImplemented s!"resource limit: fuel exhausted: {what}")

/-- Literal acceleration (the official kernel's `reduceNat`, run in the
`whnf` loop *before* delta-unfolding): pack `Nat.succ` applied to a
literal back into a literal.  Binary operations on literal arguments
are added with the verified fast-path capabilities (see DESIGN.md);
until then only the constructor packing reduces. -/
def reduceNat (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (e : Expr) :
    m (Option Expr) := do
  match e with
  | .app (.const c []) a =>
    if c = natSuccName ∧ natLitStored env then
      -- the argument is reduced first (as for the operations below):
      -- literals reach `succ` wrapped in `OfNat`/instance towers, and a
      -- missed packing here defeats the binary fast paths downstream,
      -- which then delta-grind the `brecOn` below-tower unarily
      match rawNatLit? (← r.whnf depth a) with
      | some n => pure (some (.lit (.natVal (n + 1))))
      | none => pure none
    -- (official `reduce_nat`, `type_checker.cpp:639-668`, has `Nat.succ` and the
    -- fourteen binary operations, nothing else; `Nat.pred` is a certified
    -- structural operation because `Nat.sub`'s recurrence names it)
    else pure none
  | .app (.app (.const c []) a) b =>
    if (c = natAddName ∨ c = natSubName ∨ c = natMulName ∨
        c = natPowName ∨ c = natBeqName ∨ c = natBleName ∨
        c = natDivName ∨ c = natModName ∨ c = natGcdName ∨
        c = natLandName ∨ c = natLorName ∨ c = natXorName ∨
        c = natShiftLeftName ∨ c = natShiftRightName) then
      -- Official `reduce_bin_nat_op` (`type_checker.cpp:606-614`):
      -- the FIRST argument is head-normalised and, unless it is a
      -- literal, the step fails WITHOUT touching the second.  The
      -- former two-scrutinee `match` whnf'd both up front — a cost
      -- divergence (DESIGN.md "THE DIVERGENCE AUDIT", D15): on
      -- `Nat.add o (slow n)` with `o` opaque it evaluated `slow n`
      -- where official never does (fuel death at `n = 80000`; the fixture).
      match rawNatLit? (← r.whnf depth a) with
      | some n₁ =>
        match rawNatLit? (← r.whnf depth b) with
        | some n₂ => pure (natOpResult c n₁ n₂)
        | none => pure none
      | none => pure none
    else pure none
  | _ => pure none


/-- **The full-grade telescope certificate** (task #342 L10, ι's case
3): every argument is inferred at the FULL grade, which establishes
its invariant, and its type is compared with its domain; no slot is
skipped.  The constructor spine of a case-3 redex is not a subterm of
the redex: at a valuation where the family is `Prop` the reduced major
is the point, its reduction says nothing, and only this run grades
its fields and puts them in their domains. -/
def iotaCertsFull (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) :
    Expr → List Expr → m Bool
  | _, [] => pure true
  | .forallE ty body, arg :: rest => do
    let ta ← r.infer depth arg
    if ← r.defeq depth ta ty then
      iotaCertsFull r env depth (body.instantiate1 arg) rest
    else pure false
  | _, _ :: _ => pure false

/-- Pairwise definitional equality of two spines (used to check a
major's constructor parameters against the recursor's). -/
def defEqList (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) :
    List Expr → List Expr → m Bool
  | [], [] => pure true
  | a :: as, b :: bs => do
    if ← r.defeq depth a b then
      defEqList r env depth as bs
    else pure false
  | _, _ => pure false

/-- The canonical-index comparison of a firing ι redex (the ι batch,
2026-09-05).  Where the recursor has indices (`rP < mI`) the residual of
the constructor's telescope `tyCtor` along the major's spine `margs`
must agree, past the `cnP` parameters, with the recursor's index
arguments `idx` — the model's iota equation only speaks about the
canonical indices.  At `mI = rP` there is nothing to compare (the law's
`IotaIndexPin` is discharged by `Or.inl rfl`) and the block is
skipped. -/
def iotaIndexOk (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (mI rP cnP : Nat)
    (tyCtor : Expr) (margs idx : List Expr) : m Bool :=
  if mI = rP then pure true
  else
    match piResidual tyCtor margs with
    | some residual => defEqList r env depth (residual.getAppArgs.drop cnP) idx
    | none => pure false

/-- Proof irrelevance certification: both sides' types' *sorts* are
`Prop`.  In the model everything inhabiting a proposition is the proof
point, so any two such terms are equal — no common-type check is needed: soundness holds
without it, and the check-first discipline (every subterm is
checked before definitional equality compares it; congruence compares
argument pairs only after the earlier arguments matched) makes a
heterogeneous comparison unreachable, so the official kernel's check is
implied (see DESIGN.md, design-review triage). -/
def proofIrrel (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool := do
  -- task #172 B4: every inference here is at the io grade (official's
  -- is_def_eq_proof_irrel runs infer_type — always infer_only)
  let ta ← r.inferIO depth a
  match ← r.whnf depth (← r.inferIO depth ta) with
  | .sort uT =>
    let okA ← liftFueled "level comparison" (Level.isEquiv uT .zero)
    let tb ← r.inferIO depth b
    match ← r.whnf depth (← r.inferIO depth tb) with
    | .sort vT =>
      let okB ← liftFueled "level comparison" (Level.isEquiv vT .zero)
      pure (okA && okB)
    | _ => pure false
  | _ => pure false

/-- **The hoisted proof-irrelevance test** (task #168, Option U):
`proofIrrel` behind the head-symbol fast arms — official's
`is_def_eq_proof_irrel`; the unit-like test is `stuckIrrel`'s
`structUnitCert` (official's `is_def_eq_unit_like`, the last test of
`is_def_eq_core`).

Before the io inferences, the head-symbol readers decide both fast
arms, **in both modes** (user ruling, 2026-09-06: the fast readers are
part of the real checker, and the trusted mode is the real checker
with certification-only work omitted — a reader that replaces
inference is not certification-only work).  The **"not a proof" arm**:
a side whose head-symbol datum says "not a proposition" refuses the
shortcut outright (`notProofFast`, `ConLeche/Kernel/PropRead.lean`).
Refusing is always sound — the P row is stated at `.ok true` — and the
arm's obligation is *agreement* with the slow path, recorded by the
landing census (DESIGN.md, task #168: 0 disagreements in 7.5 M calls).
The **"yes" arm** (`isProofFast` on both sides → `true`) is
justified by the squash regime, stage 3 of the same design
(`prf_of_isProofFast`, `ConLeche/Model/Rules/ProofFastSound.lean`), which the
verified mode's `DefEq.proofFast` rule consumes; the trusted mode is unverified and
inherits the arm without a row, as it inherits every other body. -/
def propIrrel (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool := do
  if notProofFast env.find? env.findTable? a || notProofFast env.find? env.findTable? b then
    pure false
  else if isProofFast env.find? env.findTable? a && isProofFast env.find? env.findTable? b then
    -- the yes arm (task #168 stage 3): both heads' data say
    -- "a proposition at every valuation" — justified by the squash
    -- regime (`prf_of_isProofFast`, `ConLeche/Model/Rules/ProofFastSound.lean`)
    pure true
  else
  -- task #172 B4: every inference here is at the io grade
  let ta ← r.inferIO depth a
  match ← r.whnf depth (← r.inferIO depth ta) with
  | .sort uT =>
    let okA ← liftFueled "level comparison" (Level.isEquiv uT .zero)
    let tb ← r.inferIO depth b
    match ← r.whnf depth (← r.inferIO depth tb) with
    | .sort vT =>
      let okB ← liftFueled "level comparison" (Level.isEquiv vT .zero)
      pure (okA && okB)
    | _ => pure false
  | _ => pure false

/-- The structure-eta certificate against the weak-head-normal type
`wtb` of the stuck side `b` (`structEtaCert` below reduces it). -/
def structEtaCertWith (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat)
    (a b wtb : Expr) : m Bool := do
  match a.getAppFn with
  | .const c us =>
    match env.find? c with
    | some (.ctorInfo cvc cnP cnF _) =>
      if a.getAppArgs.length = cnP + cnF then
        match wtb.getAppFn with
        | .const T us' =>
          match env.find? T with
          | some (.indInfo cvT caps) =>
            -- the constructor and the counts are the family's projection
            -- table's, at every field count (official: the family's
            -- constructor list)
            match env.findTable? T with
            | some tbl =>
              if caps.eta = true ∧ tbl.ctor = c ∧
                  wtb.getAppArgs.length = tbl.numParams ∧
                  us'.length = cvT.levelParams.length ∧
                  cvc.levelParams = cvT.levelParams then
                if ← liftFueled "level comparison"
                    (Level.isEquivList us us') then
                  -- no certificate on the type application: `wtb` is the
                  -- reduced type of `b`, a type, so its spine fits the
                  -- former's telescope (task #342 L11b)
                  if ← defEqList r env depth
                      (a.getAppArgs.take tbl.numParams) wtb.getAppArgs then
                    defEqList r env depth
                      (a.getAppArgs.drop tbl.numParams)
                      (etaProjs T b tbl.numFields)
                  else pure false
                else pure false
              else pure false
            | none => pure false
          | _ => pure false
        | _ => pure false
      else pure false
    | _ => pure false
  | _ => pure false

/-- **Structure η at a fully applied constructor**: `a` is a fully
applied constructor of a structure whose recorded
capabilities include eta, `b` inhabits that structure type, the
constructor's parameters are the type's arguments, and every field is
the corresponding projection (`.proj T j b`) of `b`.  The
constructor and the parameter and field counts the certificate works at
are the family's projection table's, which is what the stored law speaks; the
constructor's own counts gate the redex's shape and nothing else
(`etaCtorShape`). -/
def structEtaFull (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool := do
  -- The constructor-shape test FIRST (the divergence audit's D13):
  -- official `try_eta_struct_core` (`type_checker.cpp:824-829`) reads
  -- `s`'s head and arity syntactically and infers nothing unless they
  -- fit; ours inferred and whnf'd `b`'s type on every stuck pair, both
  -- directions, before `structEtaCertWith` looked at `a`'s head.
  if etaCtorShape env a then
    -- task #172 B4: io grade
    let tb ← r.inferIO depth b
    let wtb ← r.whnf depth tb
    structEtaCertWith r env depth a b wtb
  else pure false

/-- **The η-expansion of a partially applied constructor** (official's
`try_eta_struct_core` since lean4#15373): both sides applied to fresh
variables along the constructor side's type `ta`, one binder per missing
argument, read syntactically (official: the type of a constructor
application is the rest of the constructor's telescope, so it needs no
head normalization); at the full application, `structEtaFull`. -/
def structEtaExpand (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) :
    Nat → Nat → Expr → Expr → Expr → m Bool
  | 0, depth, a, b, _ => structEtaFull r env depth a b
  | k + 1, depth, a, b, .forallE dom body =>
    structEtaExpand r env k (depth + 1) (.app a (.fvar depth dom))
      (.app b (.fvar depth dom)) (body.instantiate1 (.fvar depth dom))
  | _ + 1, _, _, _, _ => pure false

/-- Structural eta certification (official's `try_eta_struct_core`):
`a` is a constructor application of a stored η family.  Fully applied,
`structEtaFull`.  Partially applied (`etaCtorPartial`), as official: the
two sides' io-inferred types are compared (official's
`is_def_eq(t_type, s_type)`), then both sides are η-expanded to the full
application (`structEtaExpand`).  The other side is then neutral (its
head no λ, no `let`): a λ meets the constructor through `etaCert` first,
and its io claim is not a membership. -/
def structEtaCert (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool := do
  if etaCtorShape env a then structEtaFull r env depth a b else
  match etaCtorPartial env a with
  | some k =>
    if !b.ioNeutral then pure false else
    let ta ← r.inferIO depth a
    let tb ← r.inferIO depth b
    if ← r.defeq depth tb ta then structEtaExpand r env k depth a b ta
    else pure false
  | none => pure false

/-- **Is a proposition** (official's `is_prop`): `T`'s io-inferred type
head-normalises to a sort equivalent to `Prop`. -/
def isPropType (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (T : Expr) : m Bool := do
  match ← r.whnf depth (← r.inferIO depth T) with
  | .sort u => liftFueled "level comparison" (Level.isEquiv u .zero)
  | _ => pure false

/-- **The field loop of official's `is_unit_like`**: each of the `n`
field binders of an η family's instance telescope has a proposition or
a unit-like type (`ul`, official's recursive `is_unit_like`), the field
opened at a fresh variable before the next.  The telescope is the
constructor's, so its binders are syntactic (official head-normalises
each rest, which leaves a `∀` unchanged). -/
def unitLikeFields (r : CoreFns m) (ul : Nat → Expr → m Bool) :
    Nat → Nat → Expr → m Bool
  | 0, _, _ => pure true
  | n + 1, depth, .forallE dom body => do
    if ← isPropType r depth dom then
      unitLikeFields r ul n (depth + 1) (body.instantiate1 (.fvar depth dom))
    else if ← ul depth dom then
      unitLikeFields r ul n (depth + 1) (body.instantiate1 (.fvar depth dom))
    else pure false
  | _ + 1, _, _ => pure false

/-- Recursion budget of the unit-like test (`unitLikeLoop`): the nesting
of ∀ codomains and field types it descends.  Exhaustion is not an error:
the answer is "not unit-like", which is always sound. -/
@[irreducible, noinline] def unitLikeFuel : Nat := 4611686018427387904  -- 2^62

/-- **The structure arm of official's `is_unit_like`**: `w`, a head
normal form that is no ∀, is an instance of a stored η family every
field of whose constructor, at the instance's parameters, has a
proposition or a unit-like type (`unitLikeFields`, with `ul` official's
recursive `is_unit_like`; a fieldless family has none to check); the
shape is read off the stored records (`unitLikeShape`). -/
def unitLikeStruct (r : CoreFns m) (ul : Nat → Expr → m Bool) (env : Env)
    (depth : Nat) (w : Expr) : m Bool :=
  match unitLikeShape env w with
  | some (tele, n) => unitLikeFields r ul n depth tele
  | none => pure false

/-- **Is a type unit-like** (official's `is_unit_like`, lean4#15372): its
head normal form is a ∀ whose codomain, opened at a fresh variable, is
unit-like, or an instance of a stored η family every field of whose
constructor is a proposition or unit-like (`unitLikeStruct`).  All
inhabitants of such a type are definitionally equal. -/
def unitLikeLoop (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) : Nat → Nat → Expr → m Bool
  | 0, _, _ => pure false
  | fuel + 1, depth, T => do
    let w ← r.whnf depth T
    match w with
    | .forallE dom body =>
      unitLikeLoop r env fuel (depth + 1) (body.instantiate1 (.fvar depth dom))
    | _ => unitLikeStruct r (unitLikeLoop r env fuel) env depth w

/-- **Unit-likeness** (official's `is_def_eq_unit_like`): `a`'s
io-inferred type, head-normalised, is unit-like (`unitLikeLoop`), and
`b`'s type is definitionally equal to it, so their values coincide.  A
side whose type is a ∀ must be neutral (its head no λ, no `let`): only
then is its io claim a membership (task #342 L18; a λ meets the other
side through `etaCert` first). -/
def structUnitCert (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool := do
  -- task #172 B4: io grade
  let ta ← r.inferIO depth a
  let wta ← r.whnf depth ta
  if wta.isForallE && !a.ioNeutral then pure false else
  if ← unitLikeLoop r env unitLikeFuel depth wta then
    let tb ← r.inferIO depth b
    let wtb ← r.whnf depth tb
    if wtb.isForallE && !b.ioNeutral then pure false else
    r.defeq depth wta wtb
  else pure false

/-- Eta certification for a one-sided λ against a stuck term `b`: `b`'s
type whnfs to a `∀` whose domain is defeq to the λ's, and the λ's body
is pointwise the application of `b`.  The λ is then `b`'s eta-expansion
(soundness: `SetTheory.lam_eta`). -/
def etaCert (mode : CheckMode) (r : CoreFns m) (_env : Env) (depth : Nat)
    (ty₁ body₁ : Expr) (b : Expr) :
    m Bool := do
  -- `b`'s head is no λ and no `let` (task #342 L18: its io claim is then
  -- a membership; `b` is in weak-head normal form and not a λ here)
  if !b.ioNeutral then pure false else
  -- task #172 B4: io grade
  let tb ← r.inferIO depth b
  match ← r.whnf depth tb with
  | .forallE ty₂ cod₂ =>
    if ← r.defeq depth ty₂ ty₁ then
      unless ← r.defeq (depth + 1)
          (body₁.instantiate1 (.fvar depth ty₁))
          (.app b (.fvar depth ty₁)) do return false
      -- Task #342 S0: the λ/η tax (T2)
      if mode.verifiedChecks then
        unless ← r.defeq (depth + 1)
            (← r.infer (depth + 1)
              (body₁.instantiate1 (.fvar depth ty₁)))
            (cod₂.instantiate1 (.fvar depth ty₁)) do return false
      pure true
    else pure false
  | _ => pure false

/-- The fallback for structurally distinct stuck terms: structural eta
in either direction, unit-likeness, else proof irrelevance. -/
def stuckIrrel (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool := do
  if ← structEtaCert r env depth a b then pure true
  else if ← structEtaCert r env depth b a then pure true
  else if ← structUnitCert r env depth a b then pure true
  else proofIrrel r depth a b

/-- **Official's walk to a family's sort** (the loop of `check_inductive_types`
at the use): `n` binders down, each reached by head normalization, then a sort.
`none` where the type does not get there at this environment. -/
def formerSortLoop (wh : Nat → Expr → m Expr) : Nat → Nat → Expr → m (Option Level)
  | d, 0, e => do
    match ← wh d e with
    | .sort s => pure (some s)
    | _ => pure none
  | d, n + 1, e => do
    match ← wh d e with
    | .forallE dom b => formerSortLoop wh (d + 1) n (b.instantiate1 (.fvar d dom))
    | _ => pure none

/-- **Is a family's sort never zero at the levels `us`** (official
`is_never_zero`), computed where it is asked, at the environment of the use.  A
stored type that ends in a sort needs no reduction and no opening; any other is
walked by `formerSortLoop` at the use's levels.  Where the walk does not reach
a sort the answer is the conservative one. -/
def formerNeverZeroAt (wh : Nat → Expr → m Expr) (cvT : ConstantVal) (caps : IndCaps)
    (us : List Level) : m Bool :=
  match formerResSort cvT.type with
  | some _ => pure (formerNeverZero cvT us)
  | none => do
    match ← formerSortLoop wh 0 (caps.nparams + caps.nIdx)
        (cvT.type.instantiateLevelParams cvT.levelParams us) with
    | some s => pure (Level.zeronessOf s).isNever
    | none => pure false

/-- **Case 3 of the ι case split** (task #342 L10, S5c): a rule that is not
K whose constructor's family may be `Prop` at the comparand levels `us`.
There the constructor's levels and parameters are compared with the
comparands, the constructor telescope is certified at the full grade,
and the indices are compared (task #342 L7's counterexample: at a
`Prop` valuation nothing else ties the two spines), for a `.plain` and
a `.nested` rule alike.  Elsewhere (case 2) the ι step fires with no check of
the constructor application: the family's fibres at the recursor's major domain
are strict, so the major is not the point and its reduction is an equation, and
the constructor application, being the major's value and not the point, is an
injection of fields that fit the family's constructor there.  The ι step asks
at the rule's comparand levels (`recFireComparands`), not at the constructor's,
which it does not compare there. -/
def iotaCase3At (wh : Nat → Expr → m Expr) (find? : Name → Option ConstantInfo) (isK : Bool)
    (induct : Name) (us : List Level) : m Bool :=
  if isK then pure false else
  match ctorFamily find? induct with
  | some (cvT, caps) =>
    if cvT.levelParams.length == us.length then do
      pure (!(← formerNeverZeroAt wh cvT caps us))
    else pure true
  | none => pure true

/-- **The ι case split, read off the stored facts first** (task #573): a K
rule, and a rule whose constructor's family records a sort that is never
zero (`IndCaps.neverZero`) at the comparand levels' count, need no
certificate (`iotaNoCert`), and nothing is substituted or walked for them;
any other rule is decided at the use (`iotaCase3At`). -/
def iotaCaseAt (wh : Nat → Expr → m Expr) (find? : Name → Option ConstantInfo) (isK : Bool)
    (induct : Name) (us : List Level) : m Bool :=
  if iotaNoCert find? isK induct us.length then pure false else iotaCase3At wh find? isK induct us

/-- **Is a family's sort never zero at the levels `us`**, at its recorded
fact first (`IndCaps.neverZero`), else where it is asked
(`formerNeverZeroAt`). -/
def familyNeverZeroAt (wh : Nat → Expr → m Expr) (cvT : ConstantVal) (caps : IndCaps)
    (us : List Level) : m Bool :=
  if caps.neverZero then pure true else formerNeverZeroAt wh cvT caps us

/-- Stuck-major rescue (`to_cnstr_when_K` and `to_cnstr_when_structure`
in the official kernel): a recursor's major premise that does not whnf
to a constructor application may still be *replaced* by one.  For a
K-flagged inductive proposition the parameters-only application of the
single constructor is fabricated from the major's type; its io type is
compared with the major's (official's check, unconditional) and, at the
verified mode, proof irrelevance compares it with the major (in the
model both are the proof point) — a certificate family, off at the
trusted mode (`mode.verifiedChecks`), as its cached twin gates it
through `certAtI` (`ConLeche/Cached/CoreC.lean`).
For an eta-capable structure the constructor applied to the major's
projections is fabricated with no check: the major is that application
by the family's η law (in the model both are the tuple of the major's
components).  For `And` — a proposition, which official never
η-rescues — the constructor applied to the major's projections is
fabricated and checked as in the K branch (the `And` branch below),
proof irrelevance likewise off at the trusted mode.  A
major that fails a check stays put — sound, the reduction simply stays
stuck. -/
def majorToCtor (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat)
    (_recName : Name) (isK : Bool) (rules : List RecRule) (major : Expr) : m Expr := do
  -- cheap syntactic gates before any inference: a rescue needs a
  -- single-rule recursor, K-flagged (the record's `isK`) or at an η family
  -- (`etaRescueTable?`)
  if isCtorApp env major then pure major else
  match rules with
  | [rl] =>
    match env.find? rl.ctor with
    | some (.ctorInfo cvj cnP _cnF T) =>
      match env.find? T with
      | some (.indInfo cvT caps) =>
        if isK = true then
          -- task #172 B4: io grade (lean4lean toCtorWhenK inferType)
          let tmaj ← r.whnf depth (← r.inferIO depth major)
          match tmaj.getAppFn with
          | .const T' ust =>
            if T' = T ∧ cvj.levelParams.length = ust.length then
              -- no constructor-telescope arity pin: the model proof
              -- consumes no such fact
              if cnP ≤ tmaj.getAppArgs.length then
                let fab := Expr.mkAppN (.const rl.ctor ust)
                  (tmaj.getAppArgs.take cnP)
                -- scope guard: scoping of
                -- the fabricated major is checked syntactically,
                -- keeping its verification local
                if fab.wscopedB depth && fab.looseBVarsBounded 0 &&
                    fab.fvarLeaves.all
                      (fun l => major.fvarLeaves.contains l) then
                  -- The official `to_cnstr_when_K` type check on the
                  -- fabrication: the constructor application's type
                  -- must be defeq to the major's (for `Eq` this is
                  -- the endpoint condition — `Eq.refl a : Eq a a`
                  -- against the major's `Eq a b` forces `a ≡ b`;
                  -- arena bad/098_ruleKbad fires at `Eq.rec.{3,3}`
                  -- without it).  `proofIrrel` makes the major a
                  -- proof (in the model both sides are the point).
                  if ← r.defeq depth tmaj (← r.inferIO depth fab) then
                    -- Task #342 (r1): the K check's proof-irrelevance half
                    -- is a certificate family (official stops at the type
                    -- check above) — gated off at the trusted mode, as
                    -- `certAtI mode (proofIrrelI ...)` gates its cached twin
                    -- (`ConLeche/Cached/CoreC.lean`).
                    if ← (if mode.verifiedChecks then proofIrrel r depth fab major
                        else pure true) then
                      pure fab
                    else pure major
                  else pure major
                else pure major
              else pure major
            else pure major
          | _ => pure major
        else match etaRescueTable? (env.findTable? T) rl.ctor cvj cvT caps with
        | some tbl =>
          let tmaj ← r.whnf depth (← r.inferIO depth major)
          match tmaj.getAppFn with
          | .const T' ust =>
            -- The official kernel does not eta-rescue propositional
            -- structures (`to_cnstr_when_structure` requires the
            -- structure's result sort to be provably nonzero).  The
            -- test is the *instantiated* one (task #61): a static
            -- test of the declared type's result sort passes a
            -- parametric `Sort u` that a `Prop` instantiation
            -- collapses, which is exactly the case the guard exists
            -- for.  The constructor and the counts are the family's
            -- projection table's, at every field count.
            if T' = T ∧ tmaj.getAppArgs.length = tbl.numParams ∧
                ust.length = cvT.levelParams.length then
              if ← familyNeverZeroAt r.whnf cvT caps ust then
                -- no constructor-telescope arity pin, as in the K
                -- branch, and no level-count pin: the rule's
                -- constructor carries the former's level parameters
                -- (`etaRescueTable?`), which the fabrication's levels
                -- are those of
                let fab := Expr.mkAppN (.const tbl.ctor ust)
                    (etaFabArgsE T tmaj.getAppArgs major tbl.numFields)
                -- scope guard, as in the K branch.  No certificate on
                -- the fabricated spine: the major is the fabrication by
                -- the family's η law, and the fabrication fits the η
                -- constructor's telescope by the coherence law (task
                -- #342 L11b).
                if fab.wscopedB depth && fab.looseBVarsBounded 0 &&
                    fab.fvarLeaves.all
                      (fun l => major.fvarLeaves.contains l) then
                  pure fab
                else pure major
              else pure major
            else pure major
          | _ => pure major
        | none =>
          if T = andName then
            -- THE `And`-ONLY η RESCUE (user ruling: `And` and nothing
            -- else — "this is a hack and we want its blast radius
            -- limited").  `And.rec F h` at a stuck PROOF `h` (a theorem
            -- is opaque to reduction) fires through the fabrication
            -- `And.intro a b (.proj And 0 h) (.proj And 1 h)`,
            -- certified the K branch's way: the fabrication's type
            -- against the major's, and proof irrelevance — sound
            -- because in the model every proof is the point.  Official does not η-rescue a proposition
            -- (`to_cnstr_when_structure` requires a never-zero sort),
            -- so this is an accept-superset there, reported per the
            -- `proofIrrel` ruling; it WORKS AROUND the absence of
            -- https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14925 (upstream
            -- builds `casesOn`/`recOn` of such a proposition from
            -- projections, so no `And.rec` on a proof is emitted), on
            -- top of opaque theorems, which ANTICIPATE
            -- https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14896.
            let tmaj ← r.whnf depth (← r.inferIO depth major)
            match tmaj.getAppFn with
            | .const T' ust =>
              if T' = T ∧ tmaj.getAppArgs.length = cnP ∧
                  cvj.levelParams.length = ust.length ∧
                  andRescueSlots env rl.ctor cnP = true then
                let fab := Expr.mkAppN (.const rl.ctor ust)
                  (tmaj.getAppArgs ++ [.proj T 0 major, .proj T 1 major])
                -- scope guard, as in the K branch
                if fab.wscopedB depth && fab.looseBVarsBounded 0 &&
                    fab.fvarLeaves.all
                      (fun l => major.fvarLeaves.contains l) then
                  -- the fabrication's type against the major's, then
                  -- proof irrelevance, as in the K branch
                  if ← r.defeq depth tmaj (← r.inferIO depth fab) then
                    -- Task #342 (r1): proof irrelevance here, as in the K
                    -- branch, is a certificate family — gated off at the
                    -- trusted mode (`certAtI mode (proofIrrelI ...)` in the
                    -- cached twin).
                    if ← (if mode.verifiedChecks then proofIrrel r depth fab major
                        else pure true) then
                      pure fab
                    else pure major
                  else pure major
                else pure major
              else pure major
            | _ => pure major
          else pure major
      | _ => pure major
    | _ => pure major
  | _ => pure major

/-- Convert a literal major premise to constructor form: a `Nat`
literal one layer (`litToCtorIfNat`); a `String` literal to its
*reduced* constructor form — the reference kernels re-reduce after
`strLitToConstructor` (lean4lean `Inductive/Reduce.lean`, nanoda
`str_lit_to_ctor_reducing`) since `String.ofList` is a definition, not
a constructor.  An unsupported literal passes through (stuck; sound,
and unreachable for input inference accepted, which declines it). -/
def litMajorToCtor (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) :
    Expr → m Expr
  | .lit (.strVal s) =>
    if strLitStored env then r.whnf depth (strLitToConstructor s)
    else pure (.lit (.strVal s))
  | e => pure (litToCtorIfNat env e)

/-- Convert a string-literal projection scrutinee to its *reduced*
constructor form — the references' proj expansion site (official
`reduce_proj_core`, `type_checker.cpp:383-384`; lean4lean
`TypeChecker.lean` proj clause; nanoda `tc.rs` `reduce_proj`): the
expansion's head `String.ofList` is a definition, so the whnf grinds it
to the real `String.ofByteArray` constructor form.  Only `String`
literals — no reference touches other scrutinees here.  An unsupported
literal passes through (stuck; sound, and unreachable for input
inference accepted, which declines it). -/
def projLitToCtor (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) :
    Expr → m Expr
  | .lit (.strVal s) =>
    if strLitStored env then r.whnf depth (strLitToConstructor s)
    else pure (.lit (.strVal s))
  | e => pure e

/-- Is `e` a `String` literal?  (The structure rescue leaves one to the
literal conversion, as lean4#15351's `to_cnstr_when_structure` does.) -/
def Expr.isStrLit : Expr → Bool
  | .lit (.strVal _) => true
  | _ => false

/-- The major premise's preparation before a rule fires.  Elsewhere
(the non-K case), the order of lean4#15351 (the kernel's
`inductive_reduce_rec` after Lean's kernel logic renovation,
lean4#14977): the structure rescues (`to_cnstr_when_structure`; here
also the `And` rescue) run on the major **as found**, before any head
normalization; only if they do not produce a constructor is the major
head-normalized, its literal converted, and the rescue tried once more
on the reduct.  A `String` literal skips the first attempt (lean4#15351
leaves it to the literal conversion).

Why the rescue comes first (task #342 R2, the arena's
`corner-cases/eta-struct-major-no-whnf` and
`eta-struct-major-and-no-whnf`): a structure major whose head
normalization does not terminate — `Prod.rec f loopPair`, `loopPair`
an `Acc.rec` over a proof built from Coquand's paradox; `And.rec f h`
for such a proof `h` — is rescued by reading only its *type*, and the
rule fires without the major ever being reduced.  Reducing first let
the proof's non-termination leak into the conversion of data.  The
rescues are `Red` steps on any major (`Red.rescueEta`,
`Red.rescueAnd`), so the order changes no claim; the reduct `minor
(S.p₀ x) … (S.pₖ x)` is convertible with the one plain ι gives after
the major reduces to `S.mk ps fs`.

At a K-flagged recursor the K rescue (`to_ctor_when_K`) runs on the
**raw** major — it reads only the major's *type* and fabricates the
constructor from it.  **Task #334 (maintainer ruling, 2026-10-09,
proposal P1 of the task #333 audit): if the rescue fails, the major is
left exactly as it is — no head normalization, no literal conversion.**
This deviates from the official kernel, which head-normalizes the
major unconditionally even after a failed K rescue
(`inductive_reduce_rec`'s `major = whnf(major)` runs regardless of
`to_cnstr_when_K`'s outcome).  The rescue reads only the major's
*type*: if `whnf` of a failed-rescue major ever produced `Eq.refl x`,
its type `x = x` would be definitionally equal to the recursor's
expected `a = b` by type preservation, so `a ≡ b` and the rescue would
already have succeeded.  The only loss is in incompleteness corners of
the checker's own defeq (a true `a ≡ b` that only a more complete
decision procedure — such as one that first normalizes the major —
would find); on mathlib-full the `whnf` after a failed K rescue ran
15 651 times (all `Eq.rec`) and reached a constructor 0 times (see the
TASK #333 record's audit). A K-like recursor application whose rescue
fails is simply stuck, sound either way.

The rescues (K, structure-eta, `And`) live in one function
(`majorToCtor`); the K branch is reachable exactly at the record's K flag, the
eta and `And` branches never are there (an inductive proposition fails
the eta branch's provably-nonzero guard, and `And` is not K), so the
split below dispatches each to its site.

Why the K order matters (2026-09-06, the Mathlib `decide`-over-`Rat`
frontier): with the whnf *first*, an `Eq.rec` whose major is a
theorem application — `Eq.ndrec … (Int.decEq._proof_1 a b h)` with
`h := Nat.eq_of_beq_eq_true …`, the shape `instDecidableEqRat`'s
`h ▸` produces — delta-unfolds the proofs and iota-grinds
`Nat.eq_of_beq_eq_true`'s `Nat.brecOn` tower unarily down the
`604800` literal: one knot level per `succ`, fuel exhaustion.  The
official order fabricates `Eq.refl` from the type (`a ≡ b` by the
`Nat` literal fast paths) and never opens either proof. -/
def prepareMajor (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat)
    (recName : Name) (isK : Bool) (rules : List RecRule) (major : Expr) : m Expr := do
  if isK then
    let majorK ← majorToCtor mode r env depth recName isK rules major
    if isCtorApp env majorK then
      litMajorToCtor r env depth majorK
    else
      pure majorK
  else
    let majorE ← if major.isStrLit then pure major
      else majorToCtor mode r env depth recName isK rules major
    if isCtorApp env majorE then
      pure majorE
    else
      let major₀ ← r.whnf depth majorE
      let major₁ ← litMajorToCtor r env depth major₀
      majorToCtor mode r env depth recName isK rules major₁

/-- One iota step: the expression is a stored recursor applied to
exactly its telescope (params, motives, minors, indices, major), the
major premise whnfs to a fully applied constructor with a matching
rule (a literal major converts to constructor form — see
`litMajorToCtor` —, a
stuck major may be rescued — see `majorToCtor`), and the recursor's
stored type has the redex's arity.  The constructor side follows **the
ι case split** (task #342 L10, S5c), decided at the rule's comparand levels (`recFireComparands`):
a K rule, and a rule whose constructor's family is never `Prop` there
(`iotaCase3At`), fire with no check of the constructor application
— official's `inductive_reduce_rec` checks nothing of it either; every
other rule (case 3) compares the constructor's levels and
parameters with the rule's comparands, certifies the constructor
telescope at the full grade (`iotaCertsFull`) and compares the indices
(`iotaIndexOk`).  The result is the rule's rhs applied to the non-index prefix and the
constructor's fields; over-application is handled by the outer app
recursion. -/
def iotaRec (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (e : Expr) :
    m (Option Expr) := do
  match e.getAppFn with
  | .const c us =>
    match env.find? c with
    | some (.recInfo cv mI rP isK rules) =>
      let args := e.getAppArgs
      -- checker change #9: the recursor's level arity, guarded as
      -- `unfoldDefinition` guards it (`Core.lean:174`).  The official
      -- kernel tests this immediately before instantiating the rule's
      -- RHS (`src/kernel/inductive.h:105`, present since v4.0.0);
      -- lean4lean does the same (`Inductive/Reduce.lean:98`) and
      -- nanoda's `subst_expr_levels` asserts it.  Without it
      -- `rl.rhs.instantiateLevelParams cv.levelParams us` can leak a
      -- level parameter the subject never had.  Ungated: the reference has it
      -- unconditionally, so a mode gate would break parity.
      -- task #342 L18: the stored type's syntactic arity, which the
      -- uncertified recursor spine is read against
      if args.length = mI + 1 ∧ us.length = cv.levelParams.length ∧
          (Expr.peelPis (mI + 1) cv.type).isSome = true then
        -- the major's preparation (K rescue / whnf / literal / eta) in
        -- the official order — `prepareMajor`'s docstring
        let major ← prepareMajor mode r env depth c isK rules
          (args.getD mI (.bvar 0))
        match major.getAppFn with
        | .const cj usj =>
          match env.find? cj with
          | some (.ctorInfo cvj cnP _ induct) =>
            match rules.find? (fun r' => r'.ctor == cj) with
            | some rl =>
              let margs := major.getAppArgs
              -- the parameter count is the stored constructor's, the
              -- field count the rule's; the defensive spine-length check
              -- stays
              if margs.length = cnP + rl.nfields then
                -- The ι batch (2026-09-05): the two `stripPis` arity
                -- pins that stood here were an environment invariant
                -- re-checked per fire (every install route establishes
                -- them; the P lane bound and never used them) — deleted
                -- per the "invariants over runtime gates" ruling.
                --
                -- the rule's comparands (plain: the recursor's own level
                -- instantiation and leading arguments; nested: the stored
                -- major-domain instantiations), from the firing mode
                -- computed once at its check (`sumRules` / `auxRuleFire`)
                let cmp := recFireComparands rl cnP cv.levelParams us cvj.levelParams args rP
                -- **the ι case split** (task #342 L10, #349, S5c), decided at
                -- the comparand levels: a K rule, or a family never `Prop`
                -- there (`iotaCase3At`), fires with no check, as
                -- official's `inductive_reduce_rec` (it compares nothing
                -- of the major's constructor); case 3 compares the
                -- constructor's levels and parameters with the comparands,
                -- certifies the constructor telescope at the full grade and
                -- compares the indices (`iotaIndexOk`) — a certificate
                -- family (task #342 (r1)), off at the trusted mode as its
                -- cached twin gates the whole case-3 decision through
                -- `certAtI mode (do ...)` (`ConLeche/Cached/CoreC.lean`)
                if ← (if mode.verifiedChecks then
                    (iotaCaseAt r.whnf env.find? isK induct cmp.1 >>= fun c3 => if c3 then do
                      if ← liftFueled "level comparison" (Level.isEquivList usj cmp.1) then
                        if ← defEqList r env depth (margs.take cnP) cmp.2 then
                          if ← iotaCertsFull r env depth
                              (cvj.type.instantiateLevelParams cvj.levelParams usj)
                              margs then
                            iotaIndexOk r env depth mI rP cnP
                              (cvj.type.instantiateLevelParams cvj.levelParams usj)
                              margs ((args.take mI).drop rP)
                          else pure false
                        else pure false
                      else pure false
                    else pure true)
                  else pure true) then
                  pure (some (Expr.mkAppN
                    (rl.rhs.instantiateLevelParams cv.levelParams us)
                    (args.take rP ++ margs.drop cnP)))
                else pure none
              else pure none
            | none => pure none
          | _ => pure none
        | _ => pure none
      else pure none
    | _ => pure none
  | _ => pure none

/-- **The structural projection's check** (task #175 W6): the redex
`proj_i (C p⃗ x⃗)` fires only where `C`'s stored type has the spine's
arity.  The check is the constructor telescope's syntactic arity
(`teleArityOk`, task #342 L18): the spine is a subterm of the subject,
so where the redex is not the point it fits the telescope by the
application slots of its own invariant, and a reduction claims nothing
of a redex that is the point.  The official kernel's `reduce_proj`
checks nothing.  (Level instantiation keeps the arity, so the stored
type is read uninstantiated.) -/
def projCert (env : Env) (c : Name) (args : List Expr) : Bool :=
  match env.find? c with
  | some (.ctorInfo cvC _ _ _) => teleArityOk cvC.type args
  | _ => false

/-- **The fire check as the mode runs it** (parity mirrors official,
2026-09-06): the verified core checks the constructor telescope's
arity (`projCert`); the trusted core is the official kernel's, which
reduces every constructor redex with no check.  The trusted lane stays
an accept-superset of the verified lane. -/
def projCertAt (env : Env) (verified : Bool) (c : Name) (args : List Expr) :
    Bool :=
  if verified then projCert env c args else true

/-- **The projection rule on a reduced scrutinee** (the official
kernel's `reduce_proj_core`): `proj_i (ctor p⃗ x⃗) ↦ x_i`, driven by the
projection table (never by basis names) — the table entry for
(structName, i) supplies the constructor and the counts.  A string-literal scrutinee first expands to
its reduced constructor form (`projLitToCtor`, official: `whnf
(string_lit_to_constructor c)`).  At the verified mode the fire is
certified (`projCertAt`: the constructor spine against the
constructor's stored type, task #175 W6; official certifies nothing).
`none` when the rule does not fire.  Callers: `whnfCoreBody`'s `.proj`
clause and `lazyDeltaProjReduction`. -/
def reduceProjCore (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (sn : Name)
    (i : Nat) (c : Expr) : m (Option Expr) := do
  let c ← projLitToCtor r env depth c
  match env.findProj? sn i with
  | some entry =>
    match c.getAppFn with
    | .const k us =>
      let args := c.getAppArgs
      if k = entry.ctor ∧ i < entry.numFields ∧
          args.length = entry.numParams + entry.numFields ∧
          us.length = entry.levelParams.length then
        -- the certificate runs unconditionally at the verified mode;
        -- the trusted mode runs none (`projCertAt`).
        if projCertAt env mode.verifiedChecks k args then
          pure (some (args.getD (entry.numParams + i) (.bvar 0)))
        else pure none
      else pure none
    | _ => pure none
  | none => pure none

/-- The head-normalization body: beta (with the per-redex argument
certificate, unconditional since the task-#100 de-gating), iota (with
the stuck-major machinery) and the native basis pair projection — but
**no delta**; unfolding happens in the `whnf` loop.  Values (sorts,
binders, constants, literals) return themselves.

`cheap` is the official kernel's `cheap_proj` (`whnf_core(e,
cheap_rec = false, cheap_proj)`): a projection's scrutinee is reduced
with `whnfCore` itself instead of `whnf`, and the flag propagates to
every recursive head normalization.  `whnf` runs the full mode;
definitional equality runs the cheap mode on its entry and after every
lazy-delta unfolding, so that `a.i =?= b.i` compares `a =?= b` before
reducing either scrutinee (`tests/e2e/src/proj_cheap_struct.lean`). -/
def whnfCoreBody (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) : Bool → Nat → Expr → m Expr :=
  fun cheap depth e =>
    match e with
    | .sort u => pure (.sort u)
    | .fvar idx ty => pure (.fvar idx ty)
    | .forallE ty body => pure (.forallE ty body)
    | .lam ty body => pure (.lam ty body)
    | .const n us => pure (.const n us)
    | .lit l => pure (.lit l)
    | .app f a => do
      match ← r.whnfCore cheap depth f with
      | .lam _ body => do
        -- Task #342 S0: uniform β, no certificate
        r.whnfCore cheap depth (body.instantiate1 a)
      | f' => do
        match ← iotaRec mode r env depth (.app f' a) with
        | some e'' => r.whnfCore cheap depth e''
        | none => pure (.app f' a)
    | .proj sn i pe => do
      -- official `reduce_proj`: the scrutinee by `whnf`, or by the
      -- cheap `whnf_core` in the cheap mode; then `reduce_proj_core`.
      -- When it does not fire, the INPUT is returned — its scrutinee as
      -- it was, not the WHNF computed here (official `whnf_core`:
      -- `reduce_proj` fails, `r = e`).  The WHNF has lost the
      -- scrutinee's head constant, and with it the defeq check's
      -- arguments-first comparison of `a.i =?= b.i` (lane KEEPPROJ:
      -- exponential on the self-check's `nestRoot_datF._f`,
      -- `tests/e2e/src/proj_stuck_struct.lean`).
      let c ← if cheap then r.whnfCore true depth pe else r.whnf depth pe
      match ← reduceProjCore mode r env depth sn i c with
      | some m' => r.whnfCore cheap depth m'
      | none => pure (.proj sn i pe)
    | .letE _ v b =>
      -- Task #342 S0: ζ
      r.whnfCore cheap depth (b.instantiate1 v)
    | .bvar _ =>
      throw (.notImplemented "whnf beyond the supported fragment")

/-- Step budget of the `whnfCore` head-normalization loop (task #106).
Beta, iota and projection steps are *iteration*: the interned
`whnfCoreLoopI` runs them on this budget instead of charging each step
to the shared recursion-depth budget (and to the native stack).  The
`Expr`-level specification below stays chained — the refinement bridge
reproduces a loop run by the chained recursion *at some knot fuel*
(`ConLeche/Verify/BetaSpine.lean`), so the specification and everything
above it are unchanged.

A real 104.7 GB export (a `decide +kernel` proof,
task #330) needs far more than any smaller budget, and a budget cannot weaken the check, so it is
effectively unbounded: `2^62`, the largest literal that still keeps the
decrementing counter an unboxed scalar (`2^63` is a bignum on a 64-bit
`Nat`/`LEAN_MAX_SMALL_NAT`).  A runaway input is left to an external
timeout.  See
<https://github.com/dpwoodru/general-courtade-kumar-lean/tree/main/verification/con-leche/results/2026-10-08>. -/
@[irreducible, noinline] def whnfCoreLoopFuel : Nat := 4611686018427387904  -- 2^62

/-- The outcome of one step of the cached head-normalization loop
(`whnfCoreStepI`, `ConLeche/Cached/CoreC.lean`) and of its pure mirror
(`ConLeche/Verify/BetaSpine.lean`): the head normal form, or the term
the loop continues with.  Task #342 R2: a step *returns* its
continuation instead of calling it, so every reduction step is the
loop's own tail call and a chain of any length — a non-terminating one
included — runs in constant native stack. -/
inductive WhnfStep where
  | done (e : Expr)
  | cont (e : Expr)

/-- Step budget of the `whnf` reduction loop (lean4lean's
`FuelConfig.whnf`, same value).  Literal-acceleration and delta steps
are *iteration*, not recursion: the official kernel's loop is a
`while (true)` and lean4lean's is a fixed-fuel local loop.  Task #106:
routing them through the knot instead charged every unfolding step to
the shared *recursion depth* budget (and to the native stack), so a
long-but-perfectly-ordinary unfolding chain exhausted `checkFuel`.

Task #330: effectively unbounded for the same reason as
`whnfCoreLoopFuel` above (the same real export exhausted this budget on
master before the fuel patch). -/
@[irreducible, noinline] def whnfLoopFuel : Nat := 4611686018427387904  -- 2^62

/-- Is `e` a literal? -/
def Expr.isLit : Expr → Bool
  | .lit _ => true
  | _ => false

/-- One iteration of the reduction loop (the official kernel's `whnf`
body, lean4lean's `whnf'` loop body): head-normalize, try literal
acceleration, unfold one definition — and hand the reduct to the
loop's continuation `k`.  A computed literal is the answer at once
(official `whnf`: `if (auto v = reduce_nat(t)) return *v;`): it is its
own head normal form.  A `Bool` constructor computed by `Nat.beq` or
`Nat.ble` goes to `k`, which answers it unchanged unless the
environment stores its name as a definition.  As everywhere in this
-- E1: module, the body never calls itself: the continuation is abstracted
exactly like the record `r`, so every lemma about the body is proven
once, with a hypothesis about `k`, and the loop lemma is one induction
on the budget. -/
def whnfStep (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat)
    (k : Expr → m Expr) (e : Expr) : m Expr := do
  let e₁ ← r.whnfCore false depth e
  match ← reduceNat r env depth e₁ with
  | some e₂ => if e₂.isLit then pure e₂ else k e₂
  | none =>
    match unfoldDefinition env e₁ with
    | some e₂ => k e₂
    | none => pure e₁

/-- The reduction loop: iterate `whnfStep` on its own step budget, so
the whole chain costs one knot level however many steps it takes. -/
def whnfLoop (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) :
    Nat → Expr → m Expr
  | 0, _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: whnf loop")
  | n + 1, e => whnfStep r env depth (whnfLoop r env depth n) e

/-- The reduction loop's body: run `whnfLoop` at its own step budget. -/
def whnfBody (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) : Nat → Expr → m Expr :=
  fun depth e => whnfLoop r env depth whnfLoopFuel e

/-- Ensure `e` (the type of some expression) is a sort, returning its
level. -/
def ensureSort (r : CoreFns m) (_env : Env) (depth : Nat) (e : Expr) :
    m Level := do
  match ← r.whnf depth e with
  | .sort u => pure u
  | _ => throw (.invalid "expected a sort")

/-- **The rows of a projection's guard** (`projGuardRows`, official
`infer_proj`'s `is_prop` tests): each row's term has a sort that is zero
wherever `guardSort` is, so a proposition wherever the structure is one.
`inf` is the io slot, as official's `infer_type` inside `is_prop`, in
every body. -/
def checkGuardRows (inf wh : Nat → Expr → m Expr) (guardSort : Level) :
    List (Nat × Expr) → m Unit
  | [] => pure ()
  | (d, dom) :: rest => do
    match ← wh d (← inf d dom) with
    | .sort u =>
      unless Level.zeroWhereZero guardSort u do
        throw (.invalid "projection from a propositional structure must be a proposition")
      checkGuardRows inf wh guardSort rest
    | _ => throw (.invalid "expected a sort")

/-- **The sort a projection's rows answer to**, at this use: `none` where the
guard has nothing to ask.  A structure's stored type that ends in a sort answers
at once: `Prop` asks for propositions, any other sort nothing, as official's
`is_prop` on the structure's type.  Otherwise the sort is walked to (official's
`whnf`), at the use's levels: never zero asks nothing, and a sort that may be zero
asks that each row be a proposition wherever the structure is one.  Where no sort
is reached the use is rejected, as official's `infer_type` of the structure's
type would throw. -/
def projGuardSort (wh : Nat → Expr → m Expr) (cvT : ConstantVal) (caps : IndCaps)
    (us : List Level) : m (Option Level) :=
  match formerResSort cvT.type with
  | some s => pure (if s.isAlwaysZero then some .zero else none)
  | none => do
    match ← formerSortLoop wh 0 (caps.nparams + caps.nIdx)
        (cvT.type.instantiateLevelParams cvT.levelParams us) with
    | some s => pure (if (Level.zeronessOf s).isNever then none else some s)
    | none => throw (.invalid "projection from a structure whose type reaches no sort")

/-- **The official `infer_proj` restriction** at this use (task #480):
computed here, at the environment of the use, from the stored constructor
type; nothing about it is stored. -/
def checkProjGuard (inf wh : Nat → Expr → m Expr) (env : Env) (entry : ProjEntry)
    (us : List Level) : m Unit :=
  match env.find? entry.structName with
  | some (.indInfo cvT caps) => do
    match ← projGuardSort wh cvT caps us with
    | none => pure ()
    | some guardSort =>
      match projGuardRows env.find? entry us with
      | some rows => checkGuardRows inf wh guardSort rows
      | none => throw (.invalid "projection from a propositional structure must be a proposition")
  | _ => throw (.invalid "projection from a propositional structure must be a proposition")

/-- **The argument check of a full-grade application** (task #353,
official `infer_app`): the type of an `eagerReduce _ _` argument
(`Expr.isEagerReduce`) is compared with the domain in the eager tower
(`CoreFns.defeqE`: the official kernel sets `m_eager_reduce` for this
one comparison), every other argument's in the knot's own. -/
def defeqArg (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (a ta ty : Expr) : m Bool :=
  if a.isEagerReduce then r.defeqE depth ta ty else r.defeq depth ta ty

/-- The inference body at the full grade — official's
`infer_type_core` with `infer_only = false`: every binder domain is
checked to be a type, every application argument against its domain,
and a `let`'s value against its type.  Speculative inference inside
reduction runs at the io grade instead (`inferBodyIO`). -/
def inferBody (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) : Nat → Expr → m Expr :=
  fun depth e => do
    match e with
    | .sort u => pure (.sort (.succ u))
    | .fvar idx ty =>
      -- Scope check at the leaf of a traversal that happens anyway
      -- (O(1); never a fresh walk): a free variable must refer to an
      -- enclosing opened binder.  On raw (closed) input at depth 0 this
      -- rejects any `fvar` outright; internally the checker only opens
      -- variables below the ambient depth, so for disciplined calls the
      -- check always passes (and inference success implies
      -- well-scopedness, the base case of the cache discipline).
      if idx < depth then pure ty
      else throw (.invalid "free variable out of scope")
    | .const n us => do
      match env.find? n with
      | none => throw (unknownConstError n)
      | some ci =>
        let cv := ci.toConstantVal
        unless us.length = cv.levelParams.length do
          throw (.invalid s!"incorrect number of universe levels for {n}")
        pure (cv.type.instantiateLevelParams cv.levelParams us)
    | .lit (.natVal _) => do
      if natLitStored env then pure (.const natName [])
      else throw (.invalid "Nat literal without the Nat basis declarations")
    | .lit (.strVal _) => do
      -- a string literal types as `String` (the reference kernels'
      -- `Literal.typeName`); without the pinned support declarations
      -- this is a positively detected unsupported feature: decline
      if strLitStored env then pure (.const stringName [])
      else throw (.notImplemented
        "string literals before the String support declarations")
    | .forallE ty body => do
      -- The ∀-formation rule, official-kernel style (task #100 stage 6):
      -- the codomain sort is *inferred* from the opened body.
      match ← r.whnf depth (← r.infer depth ty) with
      | .sort u => do
        let v ← ensureSort r env (depth + 1)
          (← r.infer (depth + 1) (body.instantiate1 (.fvar depth ty)))
        pure (.sort (.imax u v))
      | _ => throw (.invalid "expected a sort")
    | .lam ty body => do
      -- The domain must be a type (and the model needs its
      -- interpretation defined), exactly as in the ∀ rule.
      match ← r.whnf depth (← r.infer depth ty) with
      | .sort _ => do
        let bt ← r.infer (depth + 1)
          (body.instantiate1 (.fvar depth ty))
        pure (.forallE ty (bt.abstract1 depth))
      | _ => throw (.invalid "expected a sort")
    | .app f a => do
      let tf ← r.infer depth f
      match ← r.whnf depth tf with
      | .forallE ty body => do
        -- The per-argument check, unconditional at this grade (the
        -- soundness proof needs `⟦a⟧ ∈ ⟦ty⟧`).
        let ta ← r.infer depth a
        unless ← defeqArg r depth a ta ty do
          throw (.invalid "application type mismatch")
        pure (body.instantiate1 a)
      | _ => throw (.invalid "function expected")
    | .proj sn i pe => do
      -- A `.proj` node is typed by its projection-table entry: the
      -- stored body, level-instantiated at the subject type's levels
      -- and instantiated at its arguments and the subject (task #175
      -- S1).  Only stored table entries type bare nodes.
      let te ← r.whnf depth (← r.infer depth pe)
      match te.getAppFn with
      | .const T us =>
        match env.findProj? T i with
        | some entry =>
          -- task #175 wiring W5: the node's struct name must be the
          -- subject type's head (official `infer_proj`'s
          -- `const_name(I) == proj_sname(e)`); the readings key the
          -- table on the node's name, the checker on the head's
          if T = sn ∧ te.getAppArgs.length = entry.numParams ∧
              us.length = entry.levelParams.length then do
            -- the official `infer_proj` restriction: at a
            -- `Prop`-declared structure the field — and every earlier
            -- field a later field uses — must be a proposition at this
            -- instantiation
            checkProjGuard r.inferIO r.whnf env entry us
            -- the body at the subject type's arguments and the
            -- subject, one `instantiateList` (task #175 S1)
            pure (entry.typeAt us te.getAppArgs pe)
          else throw (.notImplemented "projection without a native entry")
        | none => throw (.notImplemented "projection without a native entry")
      | _ => throw (.notImplemented "projection without a native entry")
    | .letE ty v b => do
      -- Task #342 S0: official `infer_let`
      let _ ← ensureSort r env depth (← r.infer depth ty)
      unless ← r.defeq depth (← r.infer depth v) ty do
        throw (.invalid "let value type mismatch")
      r.infer depth (b.instantiate1 v)
    | .bvar _ =>
      throw (.notImplemented "inferType beyond the supported fragment")

/-- **The io inference body** (tasks #161, #170, #346): official's
`infer_type_core` with `infer_only = true`.  It differs from
`inferBody` at three clauses: an application's argument is not
checked against the domain, a λ's domain is not checked to be a type,
and a `let`'s type and value are not checked (the body is inferred at
the value).  The computed types and the "function expected" rejection
are `inferBody`'s.  Recursion is through `r.infer`: the io knot ties
this body to an io-grade record (`CoreFns.ioView` at the knot's io
slot, or `coreKnotIO`'s leaf lane), which is how the grade propagates
— official's `infer_type_core(e, infer_only)` passing `infer_only` to
every recursive call.

Moved here from `ConLeche/Kernel/CoreIO.lean` (task #172 B4) so the knot
can tie the io slot. -/
def inferBodyIO (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) : Nat → Expr → m Expr :=
  fun depth e => do
    match e with
    | .sort u => pure (.sort (.succ u))
    | .fvar idx ty =>
      if idx < depth then pure ty
      else throw (.invalid "free variable out of scope")
    | .const n us => do
      match env.find? n with
      | none => throw (unknownConstError n)
      | some ci =>
        let cv := ci.toConstantVal
        unless us.length = cv.levelParams.length do
          throw (.invalid s!"incorrect number of universe levels for {n}")
        pure (cv.type.instantiateLevelParams cv.levelParams us)
    | .lit (.natVal _) => do
      if natLitStored env then pure (.const natName [])
      else throw (.invalid "Nat literal without the Nat basis declarations")
    | .lit (.strVal _) => do
      if strLitStored env then pure (.const stringName [])
      else throw (.notImplemented
        "string literals before the String support declarations")
    | .forallE ty body => do
      match ← r.whnf depth (← r.infer depth ty) with
      | .sort u => do
        let v ← ensureSort r env (depth + 1)
          (← r.infer (depth + 1) (body.instantiate1 (.fvar depth ty)))
        pure (.sort (.imax u v))
      | _ => throw (.invalid "expected a sort")
    | .lam ty body => do
      -- Task #168: no domain-sort run at the io grade —
      -- official's `infer_lambda` skips it at `infer_only`
      -- (`type_checker.cpp:131`), and the model proof
      -- never consumes it: the domain's grading comes from the
      -- λ node's own `WellDenoted` premise.
      let bt ← r.infer (depth + 1)
        (body.instantiate1 (.fvar depth ty))
      pure (.forallE ty (bt.abstract1 depth))
    | .app f a => do
      let tf ← r.infer depth f
      match ← r.whnf depth tf with
      | .forallE _ body => do
        -- **THE io SITE** (task #346): official's `infer_only` — the
        -- argument is not checked against the domain; the io claim is
        -- hereditary on the type's syntax (`IORel`)
        pure (body.instantiate1 a)
      | _ => throw (.invalid "function expected")
    | .proj sn i pe => do
      let te ← r.whnf depth (← r.infer depth pe)
      match te.getAppFn with
      | .const T us =>
        match env.findProj? T i with
        | some entry =>
          -- task #175 wiring W5: the node's struct name must be the
          -- subject type's head (official `infer_proj`'s
          -- `const_name(I) == proj_sname(e)`); the readings key the
          -- table on the node's name, the checker on the head's
          if T = sn ∧ te.getAppArgs.length = entry.numParams ∧
              us.length = entry.levelParams.length then do
            -- the official `infer_proj` restriction, as in `inferBody`
            checkProjGuard r.infer r.whnf env entry us
            -- the body at the arguments and the subject, as in
            -- `inferBody` (task #175 S1)
            pure (entry.typeAt us te.getAppArgs pe)
          else throw (.notImplemented "projection without a native entry")
        | none => throw (.notImplemented "projection without a native entry")
      | _ => throw (.notImplemented "projection without a native entry")
    | .letE _ v b =>
      -- official `infer_let` at `infer_only`: the body at its value,
      -- no check of the type or the value (task #342 R-C)
      r.infer depth (b.instantiate1 v)
    | .bvar _ =>
      throw (.notImplemented "inferType beyond the supported fragment")

/-- **The eq-true shortcut** (the divergence audit's E2): official
`is_def_eq_core`'s second clause (`type_checker.cpp:1093-1101`) — when
the right side is the constant `Bool.true` and the left side has no
free variables (or the eager flag is set, task #353), the left side is
fully head-normalised (`whnf`, the
cached loop) and the verdict is `true` iff the reduct is `Bool.true`; on
failure the comparison continues.  Only the reduction is here; the
guard is `defeqBody`'s.
Verdict-neutral against lazy delta (a `whnf` reduct is what the
unfolding loop reaches, one step at a time), one memoised `whnf`
instead of one loop iteration per unfolding. -/
def boolTrueShortcut (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (a : Expr) : m Bool := do
  let w ← r.whnf depth a
  pure w.isBoolTrue

/-- Levels-and-spine congruence for two applications of the same
stored constant — the lazy delta *same-head short-circuit* (the
official kernel's `try_eq_const_app`): before unfolding both sides of
`f as ≡ f bs`, try pairwise definitional equality of the levels and
the spine arguments.  A `false` verdict is never final — the caller
falls back to unfolding — so an inconclusive level comparison simply
answers `false` here.

The arguments are compared **last to first** (task #353), as official
`is_def_eq_args` walks `app_arg`/`app_fn` from the outermost
application inwards: on `Nat.add (M+1) 1 =?= Nat.add M 2` the literals
`1 =?= 2` fail at once and both sides unfold, where a first-to-first
walk decides `M+1 =?= M` by unary successor descent first
(`tests/e2e/src/spine_arg_order.lean`).  The stuck spine congruence
(`defeqStuck`, official `is_def_eq_app`) stays first to last, as
official's does. -/
def defeqSpine (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool := do
  match a.getAppFn with
  | .const n us =>
    match b.getAppFn with
    | .const n' us' =>
      if n = n' ∧ a.getAppArgs.length = b.getAppArgs.length then
        match Level.isEquivList us us' with
        | some true =>
          defEqList r env depth a.getAppArgs.reverse b.getAppArgs.reverse
        | _ => pure false
      else pure false
    | _ => pure false
  | _ => pure false

/-- Descent budget of the binder-telescope comparison (`defeqBinders`).
Exhaustion is not an error: the level reached is compared as the
innermost one, which is always sound. -/
@[irreducible, noinline] def bindersFuel : Nat := 4611686018427387904  -- 2^62

/-- The innermost level of `defeqBinders`: the opened bodies, and for
two λ-telescopes at the verified mode their FULL-grade types. -/
def defeqBindersInner (r : CoreFns m) (lam : Bool) (depth : Nat) (b₁ b₂ : Expr) : m Bool := do
  unless ← r.defeq (depth + 1) b₁ b₂ do return false
  if lam && mode.verifiedChecks then
    unless ← r.defeq (depth + 1) (← r.infer (depth + 1) b₁)
        (← r.infer (depth + 1) b₂) do return false
  pure true

/-- One level's domains in `defeqBinders`: syntactically equal raw
domains are equal; otherwise the left one, opened over the variables so
far, against the opened right one `dom₂`. -/
def defeqDomain (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (fvs : List Expr) (ty₁ ty₂ dom₂ : Expr) : m Bool :=
  if ty₁ == ty₂ then pure true else r.defeq depth (ty₁.instantiateList fvs) dom₂

/-- **Binder-telescope congruence** (the official kernel's
`is_def_eq_binding`; the rules tier's `DefEq.forallE` and `DefEqLams`):
two ∀-telescopes (`lam = false`) or two λ-telescopes (`lam = true`) are
peeled together while both bodies are binders of that kind.  At each
level the domains are compared, unless they are syntactically equal,
each opened in one bulk pass over the variables so far (`fvs`,
innermost first); the level's variable takes the RIGHT domain.  The two
bodies are compared once, at the innermost level, each opened in one
bulk pass — and, for λs at the verified mode, so are their FULL-grade
types (the λ check).  No intermediate body is opened or compared: a
recursive `defeq` call could answer two λs by proof irrelevance or the
memo, which skips the type comparison that the outer levels rely on. -/
def defeqBinders (r : CoreFns m) (lam : Bool) :
    Nat → Nat → List Expr → Expr → Expr → Expr → Expr → m Bool
  | fuel, depth, fvs, ty₁, body₁, ty₂, body₂ => do
    let dom₂ := ty₂.instantiateList fvs
    unless ← defeqDomain r depth fvs ty₁ ty₂ dom₂ do return false
    let fvs' := Expr.fvar depth dom₂ :: fvs
    match fuel, Expr.binderPair? lam body₁ body₂ with
    | fuel + 1, some (ty₁', body₁', ty₂', body₂') =>
      defeqBinders r lam fuel (depth + 1) fvs' ty₁' body₁' ty₂' body₂'
    | _, _ =>
      defeqBindersInner mode r lam depth (body₁.instantiateList fvs')
        (body₂.instantiateList fvs')

/-- **The easy cases** (the official kernel's `quick_is_def_eq`): the
syntactic fast path, and the pairs whose verdict is decided on the spot
— two sorts (level equivalence), two literals, two `∀`s and two `λ`s
(binder-telescope congruence, `defeqBinders`).
`none` is "not an easy case".  `defeqBody` runs it on the unreduced
pair and again after the cheap head normalization, and
`lazyDeltaStep` after every unfolding.  (The
positive `is_def_eq` cache of official's version is the cached core's
`defeq` memo.) -/
def quickDefEq (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) : m (Option Bool) :=
  if a == b then pure (some true) else
  match a, b with
  | .sort u, .sort v => do
    let ok ← liftFueled "level comparison" (Level.isEquiv u v)
    pure (some ok)
  | .lit l₁, .lit l₂ => pure (some (l₁ == l₂))
  | .forallE ty₁ body₁, .forallE ty₂ body₂ => do
    let ok ← defeqBinders mode r false bindersFuel depth [] ty₁ body₁ ty₂ body₂
    pure (some ok)
  | .lam ty₁ body₁, .lam ty₂ body₂ => do
    let ok ← defeqBinders mode r true bindersFuel depth [] ty₁ body₁ ty₂ body₂
    pure (some ok)
  | _, _ => pure none

/-- `Nat.zero` or the literal `0` (official `is_nat_zero`). -/
def Expr.isNatZero : Expr → Bool
  | .lit (.natVal 0) => true
  | .const c [] => c == natZeroName
  | _ => false

/-- The predecessor of a successor form (official `is_nat_succ`): a
nonzero literal, or `Nat.succ x`. -/
def Expr.natPred? : Expr → Option Expr
  | .lit (.natVal (n + 1)) => some (.lit (.natVal n))
  | .app (.const c []) x => if c = natSuccName then some x else none
  | _ => none

/-- **Offsets** (the official kernel's `is_def_eq_offset`, the first
check of each lazy-delta iteration): two zeros are equal, two
successor forms compare their predecessors.  Two literals never reach
it (`quickDefEq` decides them first), and are left out so that the
predecessor comparison only ever pairs a literal with a `Nat.succ`. -/
def defeqOffset (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) : m (Option Bool) :=
  if a.isNatZero && b.isNatZero then pure (some true) else
  if a.isLit && b.isLit then pure none else
  match a.natPred?, b.natPred? with
  | some x, some y => do
    let ok ← r.defeq depth x y
    pure (some ok)
  | _, _ => pure none

/-- Is the head of the application spine a projection? -/
def Expr.headIsProj (e : Expr) : Bool :=
  match e.getAppFn with
  | .proj _ _ _ => true
  | _ => false

/-- Official `try_unfold_proj_app`: a term whose head is a projection
is put through the FULL `whnfCore` (the scrutinee reduced with
`whnf`); `some` whenever that changed it — also when only the
scrutinee moved and the projection did not fire. -/
def tryUnfoldProjApp (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (e : Expr) : m (Option Expr) :=
  if e.headIsProj then do
    let e' ← r.whnfCore false depth e
    if e' == e then pure none else pure (some e')
  else pure none

/-- The outcome of one lazy-delta step (official `reduction_status`):
continue with the reduced pair, or a verdict, or "neither side
unfolds". -/
inductive DeltaStep where
  | cont (a b : Expr)
  | eq
  | diff
  | unknown

/-- The end of a lazy-delta step: `quick_is_def_eq` on the new pair. -/
def deltaQuick (r : CoreFns m) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) : m DeltaStep := do
  match ← quickDefEq mode r depth a b with
  | some true => pure .eq
  | some false => pure .diff
  | none => pure (.cont a b)

/-- **One lazy-delta step** (the official kernel's
`lazy_delta_reduction_step`): read the two heads' unfoldability and
hints, unfold the side with the greater height (both at equal hints,
after the same-head argument shortcut at equal *regular* hints), put
the unfolded side through the cheap `whnfCore`, and finish with
`quickDefEq`.  With one side unfoldable, a projection application on
the other side is reduced instead (`tryUnfoldProjApp`): `expensive =?=
inst.1 a` must not unfold `expensive` lazily to the bottom.

The same-head shortcut's `sameRegular` guard mirrors the reference
kernels and is deliberate: at equal `abbrev` (or `opaque`) hints both
sides unfold eagerly instead (abbrevs are meant to unfold; a spine
attempt on abbrev-headed applications risks reduction bombs).  Do not
generalize it.  The `.unknown` exits under a successful
`unfoldableHead` are unreachable (`unfoldableHead env e` is
`(unfoldDefinition env e).isSome`). -/
def lazyDeltaStep (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m DeltaStep := do
  match unfoldableHead env a, unfoldableHead env b with
  | false, false => pure .unknown
  | true, false =>
    match ← tryUnfoldProjApp r depth b with
    | some b₂ => deltaQuick mode r depth a b₂
    | none =>
      match unfoldDefinition env a with
      | some a₂ => do
        let a₃ ← r.whnfCore true depth a₂
        deltaQuick mode r depth a₃ b
      | none => pure .unknown
  | false, true =>
    match ← tryUnfoldProjApp r depth a with
    | some a₂ => deltaQuick mode r depth a₂ b
    | none =>
      match unfoldDefinition env b with
      | some b₂ => do
        let b₃ ← r.whnfCore true depth b₂
        deltaQuick mode r depth a b₃
      | none => pure .unknown
  | true, true =>
    let ha := headHint env a
    let hb := headHint env b
    if ReducibilityHint.lt hb ha then
      match unfoldDefinition env a with
      | some a₂ => do
        let a₃ ← r.whnfCore true depth a₂
        deltaQuick mode r depth a₃ b
      | none => pure .unknown
    else if ReducibilityHint.lt ha hb then
      match unfoldDefinition env b with
      | some b₂ => do
        let b₃ ← r.whnfCore true depth b₂
        deltaQuick mode r depth a b₃
      | none => pure .unknown
    else if ← (if ReducibilityHint.sameRegular ha hb && sameConstHeads a b then
        defeqSpine r env depth a b else pure false) then pure .eq
    else
      match unfoldDefinition env a, unfoldDefinition env b with
      | some a₂, some b₂ => do
        let a₃ ← r.whnfCore true depth a₂
        let b₃ ← r.whnfCore true depth b₂
        deltaQuick mode r depth a₃ b₃
      | _, _ => pure .unknown

/-- The outcome of the lazy-delta loop: a verdict, or the pair it got
stuck on (official `lazy_delta_reduction` returns `l_undef` and updates
`t_n`, `s_n`). -/
inductive LazyRes where
  | verdict (v : Bool)
  | unknown (a b : Expr)

/-- Step budget of the two lazy-delta loops (lean4lean's
`FuelConfig.lazyDelta`, generously sized).  Exhaustion is an internal
error, never a verdict.

Task #330: effectively unbounded for the same reason as
`whnfCoreLoopFuel`/`whnfLoopFuel` above. -/
@[irreducible, noinline] def defeqLoopFuel : Nat := 4611686018427387904  -- 2^62

/-- **The lazy-delta loop** (the official kernel's
`lazy_delta_reduction`): per iteration the offset check, the literal
acceleration — guarded on both sides being free of free variables, as
in the official kernel and lean4lean (an open `Int32`/`Int64` pair
would otherwise delta-grind the `Nat.brecOn` tower; the whnf-loop
`reduceNat` stays unguarded, as the official whnf loop is), unless the
eager flag is set (`eager`, task #353: official's
`(!has_fvar(t_n) && !has_fvar(s_n)) || m_eager_reduce`) — whose
reduct restarts the comparison (`defeq`, official `is_def_eq_core`),
then one `lazyDeltaStep`.  Each unfolding is one iteration of this
loop on its own step budget (task #106: not one unit of the knot's
recursion depth). -/
def lazyDeltaReduction (eager : Bool) (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) :
    Nat → Expr → Expr → m LazyRes
  | 0, _, _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: defeq loop")
  | n + 1, a, b => do
    match ← defeqOffset r depth a b with
    | some v => pure (.verdict v)
    | none =>
    match ← (if eager || (!a.hasFvar && !b.hasFvar) then
        reduceNat r env depth a else pure none) with
    | some a₂ => do
      let v ← r.defeq depth a₂ b
      pure (.verdict v)
    | none =>
    match ← (if eager || (!a.hasFvar && !b.hasFvar) then
        reduceNat r env depth b else pure none) with
    | some b₂ => do
      let v ← r.defeq depth a b₂
      pure (.verdict v)
    | none =>
    match ← lazyDeltaStep mode r env depth a b with
    | .cont a' b' => lazyDeltaReduction eager r env depth n a' b'
    | .eq => pure (.verdict true)
    | .diff => pure (.verdict false)
    | .unknown => pure (.unknown a b)

/-- **`a.i =?= b.i` by its scrutinees** (the official kernel's
`lazy_delta_proj_reduction`): lazy-delta steps on the two scrutinees;
when neither unfolds any more (or the step found them different), the
projection is tried on both (`reduceProjCore`) and only the projected
fields are compared; failing that, the scrutinees themselves. -/
def lazyDeltaProjReduction (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat)
    (sn : Name) (i : Nat) : Nat → Expr → Expr → m Bool
  | 0, _, _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: lazy delta projection loop")
  | n + 1, a, b => do
    match ← lazyDeltaStep mode r env depth a b with
    | .cont a' b' => lazyDeltaProjReduction r env depth sn i n a' b'
    | .eq => pure true
    | .diff | .unknown =>
      match ← reduceProjCore mode r env depth sn i a with
      | some x =>
        match ← reduceProjCore mode r env depth sn i b with
        | some y => r.defeq depth x y
        | none => r.defeq depth a b
      | none => r.defeq depth a b

/-- The proj/proj check of `is_def_eq_core`: two projections at the
same slot go through `lazyDeltaProjReduction` on their scrutinees. -/
def defeqProjPair (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool :=
  match a, b with
  | .proj s₁ i₁ e₁, .proj s₂ i₂ e₂ =>
    if s₁ == s₂ && i₁ == i₂ then
      lazyDeltaProjReduction mode r env depth s₁ i₁ defeqLoopFuel e₁ e₂
    else pure false
  | _, _ => pure false

/-- **The stuck comparison** (the tail of the official kernel's
`is_def_eq_core`, after lazy delta, the proj/proj check and the full
`whnf_core` restart): the same constant at equivalent levels, the same
free variable, a string literal against `String.ofList`
(`try_string_lit_expansion`), the application spine
(`is_def_eq_app`), η on a one-sided λ; every failing arm ends in
`stuckIrrel` (proof irrelevance, structure η, unit-likeness).  Two
constants or two free variables are inert under `whnfCore`, so checking
them here, after the restart, instead of before it is the same
verdict. -/
def defeqStuck (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) (depth : Nat) (a b : Expr) :
    m Bool :=
  match a, b with
  -- a string literal against a unary `String.ofList` application:
  -- expand the literal to its constructor form and compare — the
  -- reference kernels' `tryStringLitExpansion` (lean4lean
  -- `TypeChecker.lean`, nanoda `try_string_lit_expansion`), which
  -- fires exactly when the other side's function part is the bare
  -- `String.ofList` constant
  | .lit l, .app f x =>
    match l, f with
    | .strVal st, .const cO usO =>
      if cO = stringOfListName ∧ usO = [] ∧ strLitStored env then
        r.defeq depth (strLitToConstructor st) (.app (.const cO usO) x)
      else stuckIrrel r env depth (.lit (.strVal st)) (.app (.const cO usO) x)
    | _, _ => stuckIrrel r env depth (.lit l) (.app f x)
  | .app f x, .lit l =>
    match l, f with
    | .strVal st, .const cO usO =>
      if cO = stringOfListName ∧ usO = [] ∧ strLitStored env then
        r.defeq depth (.app (.const cO usO) x) (strLitToConstructor st)
      else stuckIrrel r env depth (.app (.const cO usO) x) (.lit (.strVal st))
    | _, _ => stuckIrrel r env depth (.app f x) (.lit l)
  | .fvar i ty₁, .fvar j ty₂ =>
    if i == j then pure true
    else stuckIrrel r env depth (.fvar i ty₁) (.fvar j ty₂)
  | .const n us, .const n' us' =>
    if n = n' then do
      if ← liftFueled "level comparison" (Level.isEquivList us us') then
        pure true
      else stuckIrrel r env depth (.const n us) (.const n' us')
    else stuckIrrel r env depth (.const n us) (.const n' us')
  | .app f₁ a₁, .app f₂ a₂ => do
    -- Stuck applications: **spine-wise** congruence (the official
    -- kernel's `is_def_eq_app`, lean4lean's `isDefEqApp`, nanoda's
    -- `def_eq_app`): equal spine lengths, one head comparison, then
    -- the argument lists pairwise (task #106: never the partial
    -- applications one by one).
    if (Expr.app f₁ a₁).getAppArgs.length =
        (Expr.app f₂ a₂).getAppArgs.length then
      if ← r.defeq depth (Expr.app f₁ a₁).getAppFn
          (Expr.app f₂ a₂).getAppFn then
        if ← defEqList r env depth (Expr.app f₁ a₁).getAppArgs
            (Expr.app f₂ a₂).getAppArgs then pure true
        else stuckIrrel r env depth (.app f₁ a₁) (.app f₂ a₂)
      else stuckIrrel r env depth (.app f₁ a₁) (.app f₂ a₂)
    else stuckIrrel r env depth (.app f₁ a₁) (.app f₂ a₂)
  -- One-sided λ: eta, else the stuck fallbacks.  (Two λs never get
  -- here: `quickDefEq` decides them.)
  | .lam ty₁ body₁, b₂ => do
    if ← etaCert mode r env depth ty₁ body₁ b₂ then pure true
    else stuckIrrel r env depth (.lam ty₁ body₁) b₂
  | a₁, .lam ty₂ body₂ => do
    if ← etaCert mode r env depth ty₂ body₂ a₁ then pure true
    else stuckIrrel r env depth a₁ (.lam ty₂ body₂)
  -- Distinct whnf-stuck head symbols: only the stuck fallbacks can
  -- equate them; `false` is always sound.
  | e₁, e₂ => stuckIrrel r env depth e₁ e₂

/-- **The definitional-equality body** (the official kernel's
`is_def_eq_core`, clause for clause except one deliberate reordering):
the syntactic fast path; the `Bool.true` shortcut (E2,
`boolTrueShortcut`: right side `Bool.true`, left side fvar-free or the
eager flag set — official's `(!has_fvar(t) || m_eager_reduce) &&
is_constant(s, Bool.true)`, the flag being `eager`, task #353);
`quickDefEq` and proof
irrelevance (`is_def_eq_proof_irrel`) on the UNREDUCED sides; the cheap
head normalization of both sides (`whnfCore` with `cheap_proj`);
`quickDefEq` again; the lazy-delta loop (`lazyDeltaReduction`); the
proj/proj check (`defeqProjPair`); the FULL `whnfCore` of both sides
and, if either changed, a restart; and the stuck comparison
(`defeqStuck`).

**The deviation (task #333).**  The official kernel runs proof
irrelevance after the cheap `whnfCore`; here it runs before it, so two
proofs are never head-normalized to be compared.  Proof irrelevance
reads only the two sides' types, which reduction preserves, so the
verdict is the same on any pair whose reduction terminates; the
difference is a proof with no normal form (impredicative `Prop` +
`propext` + K-like `Eq.rec`, `tests/e2e/src/omega_kloop.lean`), which
the official order head-normalizes forever.  `quickDefEq` stays first
because it only fires on sorts, literals and binders, which `whnfCore`
leaves unchanged: on those pairs either order gives the same verdict.

The restarts — after a literal acceleration in the loop and after the
full `whnfCore` — are `defeq` calls, as official's are `is_def_eq_core`
calls, so every entry runs the prefix (proof irrelevance included)
exactly once (the divergence audit's D3 holds by construction); the
long chains — the unfoldings — are iterations of the lazy loops. -/
def defeqBody (eager : Bool) (r : CoreFns m) (env : Env) : Nat → Expr → Expr → m Bool :=
  fun depth a b => do
    if a == b then pure true else
    if ← (if b.isBoolTrue && (eager || !a.hasFvar) then
        boolTrueShortcut r depth a else pure false) then pure true else
    match ← quickDefEq mode r depth a b with
    | some v => pure v
    | none =>
    -- task #333: proof irrelevance on the UNREDUCED sides, before any
    -- head normalization (a proof is never reduced to be compared)
    if ← propIrrel r env depth a b then pure true else
    let a' ← r.whnfCore true depth a
    let b' ← r.whnfCore true depth b
    match ← quickDefEq mode r depth a' b' with
    | some v => pure v
    | none =>
    match ← lazyDeltaReduction mode eager r env depth defeqLoopFuel a' b' with
    | .verdict v => pure v
    | .unknown a₁ b₁ =>
    if ← defeqProjPair mode r env depth a₁ b₁ then pure true else
    -- the full `whnfCore` can only differ from the cheap one where the
    -- cheap mode left a projection stuck, which is then the spine head
    -- (`whnfCore` stops there); without one, the restart's test
    -- would find both sides unchanged, so it is skipped (cost only)
    if !a₁.headIsProj && !b₁.headIsProj then defeqStuck mode r env depth a₁ b₁ else
    let a₂ ← r.whnfCore false depth a₁
    let b₂ ← r.whnfCore false depth b₁
    if a₂ == a₁ && b₂ == b₁ then defeqStuck mode r env depth a₁ b₁
    else r.defeq depth a₂ b₂

end Bodies

/-- Tie the bodies together at a monad: the record whose entry points
are the bodies applied to the record one fuel level down.  Fuel is
*only* here — exhaustion is an internal error, never a verdict.  The
next level is constructed lazily, inside each entry point's closure
(constant work per call; an eager tower would cost `fuel` allocations
per instantiation). -/
def coreKnot {m : Type → Type} [Monad m] [MonadExceptOf CheckError m]
    (mode : CheckMode) (eager : Bool) (env : Env)
    (wrap : CoreFns m → CoreFns m) : Nat → CoreFns m
  | 0 =>
    { whnfCore := fun _ _ _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: whnfCore")
      whnf := fun _ _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: whnf")
      infer := fun _ _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: infer")
      defeq := fun _ _ _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: defeq")
      defeqE := fun _ _ _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: defeq")
      inferIO := fun _ _ => throw (.internal "fuel exhausted: infer") }
  | fuel + 1 =>
    wrap
      { whnfCore := fun c d e =>
          whnfCoreBody mode (coreKnot mode eager env wrap fuel) env c d e
        whnf := fun d e => whnfBody (coreKnot mode eager env wrap fuel) env d e
        infer := fun d e =>
          inferBody (coreKnot mode eager env wrap fuel) env d e
        defeq := fun d a b =>
          defeqBody mode eager (coreKnot mode eager env wrap fuel) env d a b
        -- **The eager slot** (task #353): the eager tower, the knot at
        -- `eager := true` one level down (the flag propagates to
        -- everything the comparison calls, as official's does)
        defeqE := fun d a b =>
          defeqBody mode true (coreKnot mode true env wrap fuel) env d a b
        -- **The io slot** (task #170 / #172 B4, task #346): official's
        -- `infer_only`, tied to the io-grade view of the knot one level
        -- down (the grade propagates, as official's does)
        inferIO := fun d e =>
          inferBodyIO
            (CoreFns.ioView (coreKnot mode eager env wrap fuel)) env d e }

/-- The shared fuel for the checker core: bounds the recursion depth of
reduction, inference and definitional equality.  Exhaustion is an
internal error, never a verdict.

**Effectively unbounded** (task #354).  The fuel exists so that the
knot is a structural recursion the proofs can induct over; as a limit
it is not needed: running out of it is an internal error (exit 3),
never an accept, so raising it cannot weaken the check.  It is `2^62`,
as `whnfCoreLoopFuel`.  Fuel is counted in knot levels, and each knot
level is native recursion, as in the official kernel (a `whnf` of
`W*200033` is a 200033-deep first-argument recursion in both).  What
bounds the depth is therefore the native stack: every checker thread
has the Lean runtime's default 1 GiB stack, a level costs 0.8–1.2 KB
of it, and a recursion deeper than about a million levels aborts
(`Stack overflow detected`, exit 134) — an error like exit 3 for
the arena, and never an accept.  Task #353 had sized the fuel to the
stack (500000) so that the error was a clean exit 3; the openai/math
ContinuumCoulombHardness challenge needs more than that and is
accepted on the default stack.  See DESIGN.md, tasks #353 and #354. -/
